\id XXA inferno
\usfm 3.1
\ide UTF-8
\rem anagnosis work=inferno edition=longfellow.en lang=en urn=urn:cts:anagnosis:dante.inferno.longfellow-eng cite=c.vp
\rem source Project Gutenberg 1004, https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/1004
\rem rights Public domain (Longfellow, 1867)
\rem note Who speaks each speech (zsay) is an editorial annotation, not Longfellow's.
\h Inferno
\toc1 Inferno
\mt1 Inferno
\c 1
\cp 1
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Midway upon the journey of our life
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* I found myself within a forest dark,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* For the straightforward pathway had been lost.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Ah me! how hard a thing it is to say
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* What was this forest savage, rough, and stern,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Which in the very thought renews the fear.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* So bitter is it, death is little more;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* But of the good to treat, which there I found,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Speak will I of the other things I saw there.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* I cannot well repeat how there I entered,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* So full was I of slumber at the moment
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* In which I had abandoned the true way.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* But after I had reached a mountain’s foot,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* At that point where the valley terminated,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Which had with consternation pierced my heart,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Upward I looked, and I beheld its shoulders,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Vested already with that planet’s rays
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which leadeth others right by every road.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Then was the fear a little quieted
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* That in my heart’s lake had endured throughout
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* The night, which I had passed so piteously.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And even as he, who, with distressful breath,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Forth issued from the sea upon the shore,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Turns to the water perilous and gazes;
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* So did my soul, that still was fleeing onward,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Turn itself back to re-behold the pass
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Which never yet a living person left.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* After my weary body I had rested,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* The way resumed I on the desert slope,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* So that the firm foot ever was the lower.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And lo! almost where the ascent began,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* A panther light and swift exceedingly,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Which with a spotted skin was covered o’er!
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And never moved she from before my face,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Nay, rather did impede so much my way,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* That many times I to return had turned.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* The time was the beginning of the morning,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* And up the sun was mounting with those stars
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* That with him were, what time the Love Divine
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* At first in motion set those beauteous things;
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* So were to me occasion of good hope,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* The variegated skin of that wild beast,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* The hour of time, and the delicious season;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* But not so much, that did not give me fear
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* A lion’s aspect which appeared to me.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* He seemed as if against me he were coming
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* With head uplifted, and with ravenous hunger,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* So that it seemed the air was afraid of him;
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And a she-wolf, that with all hungerings
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Seemed to be laden in her meagreness,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And many folk has caused to live forlorn!
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* She brought upon me so much heaviness,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* With the affright that from her aspect came,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* That I the hope relinquished of the height.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And as he is who willingly acquires,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* And the time comes that causes him to lose,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Who weeps in all his thoughts and is despondent,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* E’en such made me that beast withouten peace,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Which, coming on against me by degrees
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Thrust me back thither where the sun is silent.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* While I was rushing downward to the lowland,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Before mine eyes did one present himself,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Who seemed from long-continued silence hoarse.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* When I beheld him in the desert vast,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Have pity on me,”\zsay-e\* unto him I cried,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Whiche’er thou art, or shade or real man!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* He answered me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Not man; man once I was,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And both my parents were of Lombardy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And Mantuans by country both of them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘Sub Julio’ was I born, though it was late,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And lived at Rome under the good Augustus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*During the time of false and lying gods.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A poet was I, and I sang that just\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Son of Anchises, who came forth from Troy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After that Ilion the superb was burned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But thou, why goest thou back to such annoyance?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Why climb’st thou not the Mount Delectable,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which is the source and cause of every joy?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Now, art thou that Virgilius and that fountain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which spreads abroad so wide a river of speech?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* I made response to him with bashful forehead.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O, of the other poets honour and light,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Avail me the long study and great love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That have impelled me to explore thy volume!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou art my master, and my author thou,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou art alone the one from whom I took\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The beautiful style that has done honour to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Behold the beast, for which I have turned back;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Do thou protect me from her, famous Sage,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For she doth make my veins and pulses tremble.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thee it behoves to take another road,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Responded he, when he beheld me weeping,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If from this savage place thou wouldst escape;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because this beast, at which thou criest out,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Suffers not any one to pass her way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But so doth harass him, that she destroys him;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And has a nature so malign and ruthless,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That never doth she glut her greedy will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And after food is hungrier than before.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Many the animals with whom she weds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And more they shall be still, until the Greyhound\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Comes, who shall make her perish in her pain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He shall not feed on either earth or pelf,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But upon wisdom, and on love and virtue;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Twixt Feltro and Feltro shall his nation be;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of that low Italy shall he be the saviour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*On whose account the maid Camilla died,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Euryalus, Turnus, Nisus, of their wounds;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Through every city shall he hunt her down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Until he shall have driven her back to Hell,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There from whence envy first did let her loose.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore I think and judge it for thy best\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou follow me, and I will be thy guide,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And lead thee hence through the eternal place,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where thou shalt hear the desperate lamentations,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shalt see the ancient spirits disconsolate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who cry out each one for the second death;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And thou shalt see those who contented are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Within the fire, because they hope to come,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whene’er it may be, to the blessed people;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To whom, then, if thou wishest to ascend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A soul shall be for that than I more worthy;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With her at my departure I will leave thee;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because that Emperor, who reigns above,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In that I was rebellious to his law,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Wills that through me none come into his city.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He governs everywhere, and there he reigns;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There is his city and his lofty throne;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*O happy he whom thereto he elects!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Poet, I thee entreat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By that same God whom thou didst never know,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that I may escape this woe and worse,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou wouldst conduct me there where thou hast said,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That I may see the portal of Saint Peter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And those thou makest so disconsolate.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Then he moved on, and I behind him followed.
\c 2
\cp 2
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Day was departing, and the embrowned air
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Released the animals that are on earth
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* From their fatigues; and I the only one
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Made myself ready to sustain the war,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Both of the way and likewise of the woe,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Which memory that errs not shall retrace.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* O Muses, O high genius, now assist me!
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* O memory, that didst write down what I saw,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Here thy nobility shall be manifest!
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Poet, who guidest me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Regard my manhood, if it be sufficient,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ere to the arduous pass thou dost confide me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou sayest, that of Silvius the parent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*While yet corruptible, unto the world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Immortal went, and was there bodily.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But if the adversary of all evil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Was courteous, thinking of the high effect\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That issue would from him, and who, and what,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To men of intellect unmeet it seems not;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For he was of great Rome, and of her empire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In the empyreal heaven as father chosen;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The which and what, wishing to speak the truth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Were stablished as the holy place, wherein\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Sits the successor of the greatest Peter.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Upon this journey, whence thou givest him vaunt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Things did he hear, which the occasion were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Both of his victory and the papal mantle.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thither went afterwards the Chosen Vessel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To bring back comfort thence unto that Faith,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which of salvation’s way is the beginning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But I, why thither come, or who concedes it?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I not Aeneas am, I am not Paul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Nor I, nor others, think me worthy of it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore, if I resign myself to come,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I fear the coming may be ill-advised;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou’rt wise, and knowest better than I speak.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And as he is, who unwills what he willed,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* And by new thoughts doth his intention change,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* So that from his design he quite withdraws,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Such I became, upon that dark hillside,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Because, in thinking, I consumed the emprise,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Which was so very prompt in the beginning.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If I have well thy language understood,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Replied that shade of the Magnanimous,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thy soul attainted is with cowardice,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which many times a man encumbers so,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It turns him back from honoured enterprise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As false sight doth a beast, when he is shy.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That thou mayst free thee from this apprehension,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I’ll tell thee why I came, and what I heard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*At the first moment when I grieved for thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among those was I who are in suspense,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And a fair, saintly Lady called to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In such wise, I besought her to command me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Her eyes were shining brighter than the Star;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And she began to say, gentle and low,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With voice angelical, in her own language:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘O spirit courteous of Mantua,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of whom the fame still in the world endures,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And shall endure, long-lasting as the world;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A friend of mine, and not the friend of fortune,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon the desert slope is so impeded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon his way, that he has turned through terror,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And may, I fear, already be so lost,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That I too late have risen to his succour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From that which I have heard of him in Heaven.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bestir thee now, and with thy speech ornate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And with what needful is for his release,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Assist him so, that I may be consoled.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Beatrice am I, who do bid thee go;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I come from there, where I would fain return;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Love moved me, which compelleth me to speak.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When I shall be in presence of my Lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Full often will I praise thee unto him.’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then paused she, and thereafter I began:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘O Lady of virtue, thou alone through whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The human race exceedeth all contained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Within the heaven that has the lesser circles,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So grateful unto me is thy commandment,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To obey, if ’twere already done, were late;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No farther need’st thou ope to me thy wish.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But the cause tell me why thou dost not shun\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The here descending down into this centre,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From the vast place thou burnest to return to.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘Since thou wouldst fain so inwardly discern,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Briefly will I relate,’ she answered me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘Why I am not afraid to enter here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of those things only should one be afraid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which have the power of doing others harm;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the rest, no; because they are not fearful.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*God in his mercy such created me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That misery of yours attains me not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nor any flame assails me of this burning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A gentle Lady is in Heaven, who grieves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*At this impediment, to which I send thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that stern judgment there above is broken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In her entreaty she besought Lucia,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And said, “Thy faithful one now stands in need\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of thee, and unto thee I recommend him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Lucia, foe of all that cruel is,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Hastened away, and came unto the place
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Where I was sitting with the ancient Rachel.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*“Beatrice”\zsay-e\* said she, \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*“the true praise of God,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*Why succourest thou not him, who loved thee so,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*For thee he issued from the vulgar herd?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*Dost thou not hear the pity of his plaint?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*Dost thou not see the death that combats him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Lucia"\*Beside that flood, where ocean has no vaunt?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Never were persons in the world so swift
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* To work their weal and to escape their woe,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* As I, after such words as these were uttered,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Came hither downward from my blessed seat,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Confiding in thy dignified discourse,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Which honours thee, and those who’ve listened to it.’
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* After she thus had spoken unto me,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Weeping, her shining eyes she turned away;
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Whereby she made me swifter in my coming;
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And unto thee I came, as she desired;
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* I have delivered thee from that wild beast,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Which barred the beautiful mountain’s short ascent.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* What is it, then? Why, why dost thou delay?
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Why is such baseness bedded in thy heart?
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Daring and hardihood why hast thou not,
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Seeing that three such Ladies benedight
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Are caring for thee in the court of Heaven,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* And so much good my speech doth promise thee?”
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Even as the flowerets, by nocturnal chill,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Bowed down and closed, when the sun whitens them,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Uplift themselves all open on their stems;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Such I became with my exhausted strength,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And such good courage to my heart there coursed,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* That I began, like an intrepid person:
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O she compassionate, who succoured me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And courteous thou, who hast obeyed so soon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The words of truth which she addressed to thee!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou hast my heart so with desire disposed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To the adventure, with these words of thine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That to my first intent I have returned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Now go, for one sole will is in us both,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou Leader, and thou Lord, and Master thou.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Thus said I to him; and when he had moved,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* I entered on the deep and savage way.
\c 3
\cp 3
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*“Through me the way is to the city dolent;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Through me the way is to eternal dole;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Through me the way among the people lost.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Justice incited my sublime Creator;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Created me divine Omnipotence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*The highest Wisdom and the primal Love.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Before me there were no created things,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*Only eterne, and I eternal last.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="The gate"\*All hope abandon, ye who enter in!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* These words in sombre colour I beheld
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Written upon the summit of a gate;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Whence I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Their sense is, Master, hard to me!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And he to me, as one experienced:
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Here all suspicion needs must be abandoned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*All cowardice must needs be here extinct.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*We to the place have come, where I have told thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou shalt behold the people dolorous\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who have foregone the good of intellect.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And after he had laid his hand on mine
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* With joyful mien, whence I was comforted,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* He led me in among the secret things.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* There sighs, complaints, and ululations loud
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Resounded through the air without a star,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Whence I, at the beginning, wept thereat.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Languages diverse, horrible dialects,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Accents of anger, words of agony,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* And voices high and hoarse, with sound of hands,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Made up a tumult that goes whirling on
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* For ever in that air for ever black,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Even as the sand doth, when the whirlwind breathes.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And I, who had my head with horror bound,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, what is this which now I hear?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What folk is this, which seems by pain so vanquished?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This miserable mode\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Maintain the melancholy souls of those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who lived withouten infamy or praise.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Commingled are they with that caitiff choir\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of Angels, who have not rebellious been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nor faithful were to God, but were for self.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The heavens expelled them, not to be less fair;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nor them the nethermore abyss receives,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For glory none the damned would have from them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Master, what so grievous is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To these, that maketh them lament so sore?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* He answered: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I will tell thee very briefly.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*These have no longer any hope of death;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And this blind life of theirs is so debased,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They envious are of every other fate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No fame of them the world permits to be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Misericord and Justice both disdain them.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let us not speak of them, but look, and pass.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I, who looked again, beheld a banner,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Which, whirling round, ran on so rapidly,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* That of all pause it seemed to me indignant;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And after it there came so long a train
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Of people, that I ne’er would have believed
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* That ever Death so many had undone.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* When some among them I had recognised,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* I looked, and I beheld the shade of him
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Who made through cowardice the great refusal.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Forthwith I comprehended, and was certain,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* That this the sect was of the caitiff wretches
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Hateful to God and to his enemies.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* These miscreants, who never were alive,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Were naked, and were stung exceedingly
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* By gadflies and by hornets that were there.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* These did their faces irrigate with blood,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Which, with their tears commingled, at their feet
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* By the disgusting worms was gathered up.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And when to gazing farther I betook me.
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* People I saw on a great river’s bank;
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Whence said I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, now vouchsafe to me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That I may know who these are, and what law\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Makes them appear so ready to pass over,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As I discern athwart the dusky light.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“These things shall all be known\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To thee, as soon as we our footsteps stay\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon the dismal shore of Acheron.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Then with mine eyes ashamed and downward cast,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Fearing my words might irksome be to him,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* From speech refrained I till we reached the river.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And lo! towards us coming in a boat
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* An old man, hoary with the hair of eld,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Crying: \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*“Woe unto you, ye souls depraved!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*Hope nevermore to look upon the heavens;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*I come to lead you to the other shore,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*To the eternal shades in heat and frost.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*And thou, that yonder standest, living soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*Withdraw thee from these people, who are dead!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* But when he saw that I did not withdraw,
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* He said: \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*“By other ways, by other ports\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*Thou to the shore shalt come, not here, for passage;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Charon"\*A lighter vessel needs must carry thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And unto him the Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Vex thee not, Charon;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It is so willed there where is power to do\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That which is willed; and farther question not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Thereat were quieted the fleecy cheeks
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Of him the ferryman of the livid fen,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Who round about his eyes had wheels of flame.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* But all those souls who weary were and naked
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Their colour changed and gnashed their teeth together,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* As soon as they had heard those cruel words.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* God they blasphemed and their progenitors,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The human race, the place, the time, the seed
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Of their engendering and of their birth!
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Thereafter all together they drew back,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Bitterly weeping, to the accursed shore,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Which waiteth every man who fears not God.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Charon the demon, with the eyes of glede,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Beckoning to them, collects them all together,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Beats with his oar whoever lags behind.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* As in the autumn-time the leaves fall off,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* First one and then another, till the branch
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Unto the earth surrenders all its spoils;
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* In similar wise the evil seed of Adam
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Throw themselves from that margin one by one,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* At signals, as a bird unto its lure.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* So they depart across the dusky wave,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* And ere upon the other side they land,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Again on this side a new troop assembles.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“My son,”\zsay-e\* the courteous Master said to me,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“All those who perish in the wrath of God\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here meet together out of every land;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And ready are they to pass o’er the river,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because celestial Justice spurs them on,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that their fear is turned into desire.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This way there never passes a good soul;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And hence if Charon doth complain of thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Well mayst thou know now what his speech imports.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* This being finished, all the dusk champaign
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Trembled so violently, that of that terror
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* The recollection bathes me still with sweat.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* The land of tears gave forth a blast of wind,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* And fulminated a vermilion light,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Which overmastered in me every sense,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And as a man whom sleep hath seized I fell.
\c 4
\cp 4
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Broke the deep lethargy within my head
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* A heavy thunder, so that I upstarted,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Like to a person who by force is wakened;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And round about I moved my rested eyes,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Uprisen erect, and steadfastly I gazed,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* To recognise the place wherein I was.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* True is it, that upon the verge I found me
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Of the abysmal valley dolorous,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* That gathers thunder of infinite ululations.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Obscure, profound it was, and nebulous,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* So that by fixing on its depths my sight
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Nothing whatever I discerned therein.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let us descend now into the blind world,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Began the Poet, pallid utterly;
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I will be first, and thou shalt second be.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And I, who of his colour was aware,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“How shall I come, if thou art afraid,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who’rt wont to be a comfort to my fears?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The anguish of the people\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who are below here in my face depicts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That pity which for terror thou hast taken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let us go on, for the long way impels us.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Thus he went in, and thus he made me enter
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* The foremost circle that surrounds the abyss.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* There, as it seemed to me from listening,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Were lamentations none, but only sighs,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That tremble made the everlasting air.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And this arose from sorrow without torment,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Which the crowds had, that many were and great,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Of infants and of women and of men.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* To me the Master good: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou dost not ask\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What spirits these, which thou beholdest, are?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now will I have thee know, ere thou go farther,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That they sinned not; and if they merit had,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis not enough, because they had not baptism\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which is the portal of the Faith thou holdest;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if they were before Christianity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In the right manner they adored not God;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And among such as these am I myself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For such defects, and not for other guilt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Lost are we and are only so far punished,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That without hope we live on in desire.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Great grief seized on my heart when this I heard,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Because some people of much worthiness
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* I knew, who in that Limbo were suspended.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Tell me, my Master, tell me, thou my Lord,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Began I, with desire of being certain
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Of that Faith which o’ercometh every error,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Came any one by his own merit hence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or by another’s, who was blessed thereafter?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And he, who understood my covert speech,
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Replied: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I was a novice in this state,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When I saw hither come a Mighty One,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With sign of victory incoronate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hence he drew forth the shade of the First Parent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And that of his son Abel, and of Noah,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of Moses the lawgiver, and the obedient\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Abraham, patriarch, and David, king,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Israel with his father and his children,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And Rachel, for whose sake he did so much,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And others many, and he made them blessed;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And thou must know, that earlier than these\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Never were any human spirits saved.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* We ceased not to advance because he spake,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* But still were passing onward through the forest,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* The forest, say I, of thick-crowded ghosts.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Not very far as yet our way had gone
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* This side the summit, when I saw a fire
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* That overcame a hemisphere of darkness.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* We were a little distant from it still,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* But not so far that I in part discerned not
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* That honourable people held that place.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O thou who honourest every art and science,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who may these be, which such great honour have,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That from the fashion of the rest it parts them?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The honourable name,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That sounds of them above there in thy life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Wins grace in Heaven, that so advances them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* In the mean time a voice was heard by me:
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“All honour be to the pre-eminent Poet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*His shade returns again, that was departed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* After the voice had ceased and quiet was,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Four mighty shades I saw approaching us;
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Semblance had they nor sorrowful nor glad.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* To say to me began my gracious Master:
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Him with that falchion in his hand behold,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who comes before the three, even as their lord.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That one is Homer, Poet sovereign;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He who comes next is Horace, the satirist;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The third is Ovid, and the last is Lucan.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because to each of these with me applies\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The name that solitary voice proclaimed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They do me honour, and in that do well.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Thus I beheld assemble the fair school
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Of that lord of the song pre-eminent,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Who o’er the others like an eagle soars.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* When they together had discoursed somewhat,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* They turned to me with signs of salutation,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And on beholding this, my Master smiled;
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And more of honour still, much more, they did me,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* In that they made me one of their own band;
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* So that the sixth was I, ’mid so much wit.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Thus we went on as far as to the light,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Things saying ’tis becoming to keep silent,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* As was the saying of them where I was.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* We came unto a noble castle’s foot,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Seven times encompassed with lofty walls,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Defended round by a fair rivulet;
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* This we passed over even as firm ground;
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Through portals seven I entered with these Sages;
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* We came into a meadow of fresh verdure.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* People were there with solemn eyes and slow,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Of great authority in their countenance;
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* They spake but seldom, and with gentle voices.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Thus we withdrew ourselves upon one side
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Into an opening luminous and lofty,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* So that they all of them were visible.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* There opposite, upon the green enamel,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Were pointed out to me the mighty spirits,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Whom to have seen I feel myself exalted.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* I saw Electra with companions many,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* ’Mongst whom I knew both Hector and Aeneas,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Caesar in armour with gerfalcon eyes;
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* I saw Camilla and Penthesilea
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* On the other side, and saw the King Latinus,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Who with Lavinia his daughter sat;
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* I saw that Brutus who drove Tarquin forth,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Lucretia, Julia, Marcia, and Cornelia,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* And saw alone, apart, the Saladin.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* When I had lifted up my brows a little,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* The Master I beheld of those who know,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Sit with his philosophic family.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* All gaze upon him, and all do him honour.
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* There I beheld both Socrates and Plato,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Who nearer him before the others stand;
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Democritus, who puts the world on chance,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Diogenes, Anaxagoras, and Thales,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Zeno, Empedocles, and Heraclitus;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Of qualities I saw the good collector,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Hight Dioscorides; and Orpheus saw I,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Tully and Livy, and moral Seneca,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* Euclid, geometrician, and Ptolemy,
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Galen, Hippocrates, and Avicenna,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Averroes, who the great Comment made.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* I cannot all of them pourtray in full,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Because so drives me onward the long theme,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* That many times the word comes short of fact.
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* The sixfold company in two divides;
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* Another way my sapient Guide conducts me
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* Forth from the quiet to the air that trembles;
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And to a place I come where nothing shines.
\c 5
\cp 5
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Thus I descended out of the first circle
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Down to the second, that less space begirds,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And so much greater dole, that goads to wailing.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* There standeth Minos horribly, and snarls;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Examines the transgressions at the entrance;
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Judges, and sends according as he girds him.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* I say, that when the spirit evil-born
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Cometh before him, wholly it confesses;
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And this discriminator of transgressions
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Seeth what place in Hell is meet for it;
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Girds himself with his tail as many times
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* As grades he wishes it should be thrust down.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Always before him many of them stand;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* They go by turns each one unto the judgment;
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* They speak, and hear, and then are downward hurled.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Minos"\*“O thou, that to this dolorous hostelry\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Minos"\*Comest,”\zsay-e\* said Minos to me, when he saw me,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Leaving the practice of so great an office,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Minos"\*“Look how thou enterest, and in whom thou trustest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Minos"\*Let not the portal’s amplitude deceive thee.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And unto him my Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Why criest thou too?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do not impede his journey fate-ordained;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It is so willed there where is power to do\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That which is willed; and ask no further question.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And now begin the dolesome notes to grow
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Audible unto me; now am I come
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* There where much lamentation strikes upon me.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* I came into a place mute of all light,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Which bellows as the sea does in a tempest,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* If by opposing winds ’t is combated.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* The infernal hurricane that never rests
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Hurtles the spirits onward in its rapine;
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Whirling them round, and smiting, it molests them.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* When they arrive before the precipice,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* There are the shrieks, the plaints, and the laments,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* There they blaspheme the puissance divine.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I understood that unto such a torment
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* The carnal malefactors were condemned,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Who reason subjugate to appetite.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And as the wings of starlings bear them on
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* In the cold season in large band and full,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* So doth that blast the spirits maledict;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* It hither, thither, downward, upward, drives them;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* No hope doth comfort them for evermore,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Not of repose, but even of lesser pain.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And as the cranes go chanting forth their lays,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Making in air a long line of themselves,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* So saw I coming, uttering lamentations,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Shadows borne onward by the aforesaid stress.
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Whereupon said I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, who are those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*People, whom the black air so castigates?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The first of those, of whom intelligence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou fain wouldst have,”\zsay-e\* then said he unto me,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The empress was of many languages.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To sensual vices she was so abandoned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That lustful she made licit in her law,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To remove the blame to which she had been led.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She is Semiramis, of whom we read\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That she succeeded Ninus, and was his spouse;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She held the land which now the Sultan rules.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The next is she who killed herself for love,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And broke faith with the ashes of Sichaeus;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then Cleopatra the voluptuous.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Helen I saw, for whom so many ruthless
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Seasons revolved; and saw the great Achilles,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Who at the last hour combated with Love.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Paris I saw, Tristan; and more than a thousand
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Shades did he name and point out with his finger,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Whom Love had separated from our life.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* After that I had listened to my Teacher,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Naming the dames of eld and cavaliers,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Pity prevailed, and I was nigh bewildered.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Poet, willingly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Speak would I to those two, who go together,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And seem upon the wind to be so light.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And, he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou’lt mark, when they shall be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nearer to us; and then do thou implore them\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By love which leadeth them, and they will come.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Soon as the wind in our direction sways them,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* My voice uplift I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O ye weary souls!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Come speak to us, if no one interdicts it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* As turtle-doves, called onward by desire,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* With open and steady wings to the sweet nest
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Fly through the air by their volition borne,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* So came they from the band where Dido is,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Approaching us athwart the air malign,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* So strong was the affectionate appeal.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*“O living creature gracious and benignant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Who visiting goest through the purple air\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Us, who have stained the world incarnadine,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*If were the King of the Universe our friend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*We would pray unto him to give thee peace,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Since thou hast pity on our woe perverse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Of what it pleases thee to hear and speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*That will we hear, and we will speak to you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*While silent is the wind, as it is now.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Sitteth the city, wherein I was born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Upon the sea-shore where the Po descends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*To rest in peace with all his retinue.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Love, that on gentle heart doth swiftly seize,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Seized this man for the person beautiful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*That was ta’en from me, and still the mode offends me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Love, that exempts no one beloved from loving,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Seized me with pleasure of this man so strongly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*That, as thou seest, it doth not yet desert me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Love has conducted us unto one death;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Caina waiteth him who quenched our life!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* These words were borne along from them to us.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* As soon as I had heard those souls tormented,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* I bowed my face, and so long held it down
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Until the Poet said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“What thinkest?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* When I made answer, I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Alas!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*How many pleasant thoughts, how much desire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Conducted these unto the dolorous pass!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Then unto them I turned me, and I spake,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* And I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thine agonies, Francesca,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Sad and compassionate to weeping make me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, at the time of those sweet sighs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By what and in what manner Love conceded,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That you should know your dubious desires?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And she to me: \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*“There is no greater sorrow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Than to be mindful of the happy time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*In misery, and that thy Teacher knows.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*But, if to recognise the earliest root\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Of love in us thou hast so great desire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*I will do even as he who weeps and speaks.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*One day we reading were for our delight\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Of Launcelot, how Love did him enthral.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Alone we were and without any fear.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Full many a time our eyes together drew\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*That reading, and drove the colour from our faces;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*But one point only was it that o’ercame us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*When as we read of the much-longed-for smile\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Being by such a noble lover kissed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*This one, who ne’er from me shall be divided,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Kissed me upon the mouth all palpitating.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*Galeotto was the book and he who wrote it.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesca"\*That day no farther did we read therein.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* And all the while one spirit uttered this,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* The other one did weep so, that, for pity,
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* I swooned away as if I had been dying,
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* And fell, even as a dead body falls.
\c 6
\cp 6
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* At the return of consciousness, that closed
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Before the pity of those two relations,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Which utterly with sadness had confused me,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* New torments I behold, and new tormented
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Around me, whichsoever way I move,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And whichsoever way I turn, and gaze.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* In the third circle am I of the rain
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Eternal, maledict, and cold, and heavy;
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Its law and quality are never new.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Huge hail, and water sombre-hued, and snow,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Athwart the tenebrous air pour down amain;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Noisome the earth is, that receiveth this.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Cerberus, monster cruel and uncouth,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* With his three gullets like a dog is barking
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Over the people that are there submerged.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Red eyes he has, and unctuous beard and black,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And belly large, and armed with claws his hands;
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* He rends the spirits, flays, and quarters them.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Howl the rain maketh them like unto dogs;
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* One side they make a shelter for the other;
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Oft turn themselves the wretched reprobates.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* When Cerberus perceived us, the great worm!
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* His mouths he opened, and displayed his tusks;
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Not a limb had he that was motionless.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And my Conductor, with his spans extended,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Took of the earth, and with his fists well filled,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* He threw it into those rapacious gullets.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Such as that dog is, who by barking craves,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And quiet grows soon as his food he gnaws,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* For to devour it he but thinks and struggles,
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* The like became those muzzles filth-begrimed
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Of Cerberus the demon, who so thunders
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Over the souls that they would fain be deaf.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* We passed across the shadows, which subdues
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* The heavy rain-storm, and we placed our feet
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Upon their vanity that person seems.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* They all were lying prone upon the earth,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Excepting one, who sat upright as soon
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* As he beheld us passing on before him.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*“O thou that art conducted through this Hell,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* He said to me, \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*“recall me, if thou canst;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Thyself wast made before I was unmade.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The anguish which thou hast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Perhaps doth draw thee out of my remembrance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that it seems not I have ever seen thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me who thou art, that in so doleful\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*A place art put, and in such punishment,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If some are greater, none is so displeasing.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*“Thy city, which is full\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Of envy so that now the sack runs over,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Held me within it in the life serene.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*You citizens were wont to call me Ciacco;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*For the pernicious sin of gluttony\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*I, as thou seest, am battered by this rain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*And I, sad soul, am not the only one,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*For all these suffer the like penalty\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*For the like sin;”\zsay-e\* and word no more spake he.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* I answered him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ciacco, thy wretchedness\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Weighs on me so that it to weep invites me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, if thou knowest, to what shall come\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The citizens of the divided city;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If any there be just; and the occasion\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me why so much discord has assailed it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*“They, after long contention,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Will come to bloodshed; and the rustic party\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Will drive the other out with much offence.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Then afterwards behoves it this one fall\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Within three suns, and rise again the other\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*By force of him who now is on the coast.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*High will it hold its forehead a long while,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Keeping the other under heavy burdens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Howe’er it weeps thereat and is indignant.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*The just are two, and are not understood there;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Envy and Arrogance and Avarice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*Are the three sparks that have all hearts enkindled.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Here ended he his tearful utterance;
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I wish thee still to teach me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And make a gift to me of further speech.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Farinata and Tegghiaio, once so worthy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Jacopo Rusticucci, Arrigo, and Mosca,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And others who on good deeds set their thoughts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Say where they are, and cause that I may know them;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For great desire constraineth me to learn\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If Heaven doth sweeten them, or Hell envenom.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*“They are among the blacker souls;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*A different sin downweighs them to the bottom;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*If thou so far descendest, thou canst see them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*But when thou art again in the sweet world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*I pray thee to the mind of others bring me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciacco"\*No more I tell thee and no more I answer.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then his straightforward eyes he turned askance,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Eyed me a little, and then bowed his head;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* He fell therewith prone like the other blind.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And the Guide said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“He wakes no more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This side the sound of the angelic trumpet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When shall approach the hostile Potentate,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Each one shall find again his dismal tomb,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall reassume his flesh and his own figure,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall hear what through eternity re-echoes.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* So we passed onward o’er the filthy mixture
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Of shadows and of rain with footsteps slow,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Touching a little on the future life.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Wherefore I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, these torments here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Will they increase after the mighty sentence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or lesser be, or will they be as burning?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Return unto thy science,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which wills, that as the thing more perfect is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The more it feels of pleasure and of pain.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Albeit that this people maledict\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To true perfection never can attain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hereafter more than now they look to be.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Round in a circle by that road we went,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Speaking much more, which I do not repeat;
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* We came unto the point where the descent is;
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* There we found Plutus the great enemy.
\c 7
\cp 7
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Plutus"\*“Pape Satan, Pape Satan, Aleppe!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Thus Plutus with his clucking voice began;
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And that benignant Sage, who all things knew,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Said, to encourage me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let not thy fear\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Harm thee; for any power that he may have\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shall not prevent thy going down this crag.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Then he turned round unto that bloated lip,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be silent, thou accursed wolf;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Consume within thyself with thine own rage.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Not causeless is this journey to the abyss;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thus is it willed on high, where Michael wrought\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Vengeance upon the proud adultery.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Even as the sails inflated by the wind
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Involved together fall when snaps the mast,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* So fell the cruel monster to the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Thus we descended into the fourth chasm,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Gaining still farther on the dolesome shore
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which all the woe of the universe insacks.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Justice of God, ah! who heaps up so many
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* New toils and sufferings as I beheld?
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And why doth our transgression waste us so?
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As doth the billow there upon Charybdis,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* That breaks itself on that which it encounters,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* So here the folk must dance their roundelay.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Here saw I people, more than elsewhere, many,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* On one side and the other, with great howls,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Rolling weights forward by main force of chest.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* They clashed together, and then at that point
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Each one turned backward, rolling retrograde,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Crying, \zsay-s |who="The prodigals"\*“Why keepest?”\zsay-e\* and, \zsay-s |who="The misers"\*“Why squanderest thou?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Thus they returned along the lurid circle
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* On either hand unto the opposite point,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Shouting their shameful metre evermore.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Then each, when he arrived there, wheeled about
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Through his half-circle to another joust;
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And I, who had my heart pierced as it were,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Exclaimed: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, now declare to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What people these are, and if all were clerks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*These shaven crowns upon the left of us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“All of them were asquint\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In intellect in the first life, so much\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That there with measure they no spending made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Clearly enough their voices bark it forth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whene’er they reach the two points of the circle,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where sunders them the opposite defect.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Clerks those were who no hairy covering\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Have on the head, and Popes and Cardinals,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In whom doth Avarice practise its excess.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, among such as these\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I ought forsooth to recognise some few,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who were infected with these maladies.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Vain thought thou entertainest;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The undiscerning life which made them sordid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now makes them unto all discernment dim.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Forever shall they come to these two buttings;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*These from the sepulchre shall rise again\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With the fist closed, and these with tresses shorn.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ill giving and ill keeping the fair world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Have ta’en from them, and placed them in this scuffle;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whate’er it be, no words adorn I for it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now canst thou, Son, behold the transient farce\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of goods that are committed unto Fortune,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For which the human race each other buffet;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For all the gold that is beneath the moon,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or ever has been, of these weary souls\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Could never make a single one repose.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master,”\zsay-e\* I said to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“now tell me also\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What is this Fortune which thou speakest of,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That has the world’s goods so within its clutches?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O creatures imbecile,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What ignorance is this which doth beset you?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now will I have thee learn my judgment of her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He whose omniscience everything transcends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The heavens created, and gave who should guide them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That every part to every part may shine,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Distributing the light in equal measure;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He in like manner to the mundane splendours\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ordained a general ministress and guide,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That she might change at times the empty treasures\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From race to race, from one blood to another,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Beyond resistance of all human wisdom.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore one people triumphs, and another\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Languishes, in pursuance of her judgment,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which hidden is, as in the grass a serpent.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Your knowledge has no counterstand against her;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She makes provision, judges, and pursues\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Her governance, as theirs the other gods.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Her permutations have not any truce;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Necessity makes her precipitate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So often cometh who his turn obtains.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And this is she who is so crucified\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Even by those who ought to give her praise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Giving her blame amiss, and bad repute.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But she is blissful, and she hears it not;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among the other primal creatures gladsome\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She turns her sphere, and blissful she rejoices.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let us descend now unto greater woe;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Already sinks each star that was ascending\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When I set out, and loitering is forbidden.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* We crossed the circle to the other bank,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Near to a fount that boils, and pours itself
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Along a gully that runs out of it.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* The water was more sombre far than perse;
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And we, in company with the dusky waves,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Made entrance downward by a path uncouth.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* A marsh it makes, which has the name of Styx,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* This tristful brooklet, when it has descended
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Down to the foot of the malign gray shores.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And I, who stood intent upon beholding,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Saw people mud-besprent in that lagoon,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* All of them naked and with angry look.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* They smote each other not alone with hands,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* But with the head and with the breast and feet,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Tearing each other piecemeal with their teeth.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Said the good Master: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Son, thou now beholdest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The souls of those whom anger overcame;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And likewise I would have thee know for certain\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Beneath the water people are who sigh\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And make this water bubble at the surface,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As the eye tells thee wheresoe’er it turns.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fixed in the mire they say, ‘We sullen were\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In the sweet air, which by the sun is gladdened,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bearing within ourselves the sluggish reek;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now we are sullen in this sable mire.’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This hymn do they keep gurgling in their throats,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For with unbroken words they cannot say it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Thus we went circling round the filthy fen
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* A great arc ’twixt the dry bank and the swamp,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* With eyes turned unto those who gorge the mire;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Unto the foot of a tower we came at last.
\c 8
\cp 8
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* I say, continuing, that long before
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* We to the foot of that high tower had come,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Our eyes went upward to the summit of it,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* By reason of two flamelets we saw placed there,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And from afar another answer them,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* So far, that hardly could the eye attain it.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And, to the sea of all discernment turned,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What sayeth this, and what respondeth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That other fire? and who are they that made it?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Across the turbid waves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What is expected thou canst now discern,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If reek of the morass conceal it not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Cord never shot an arrow from itself
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* That sped away athwart the air so swift,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* As I beheld a very little boat
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Come o’er the water tow’rds us at that moment,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Under the guidance of a single pilot,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Who shouted, \zsay-s |who="Phlegyas"\*“Now art thou arrived, fell soul?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Phlegyas, Phlegyas, thou criest out in vain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For this once,”\zsay-e\* said my Lord; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“thou shalt not have us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Longer than in the passing of the slough.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As he who listens to some great deceit
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* That has been done to him, and then resents it,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Such became Phlegyas, in his gathered wrath.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* My Guide descended down into the boat,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And then he made me enter after him,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* And only when I entered seemed it laden.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Soon as the Guide and I were in the boat,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* The antique prow goes on its way, dividing
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* More of the water than ’tis wont with others.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* While we were running through the dead canal,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Uprose in front of me one full of mire,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And said, \zsay-s |who="Filippo Argenti"\*“Who ’rt thou that comest ere the hour?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Although I come, I stay not;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But who art thou that hast become so squalid?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Filippo Argenti"\*“Thou seest that I am one who weeps,”\zsay-e\* he answered.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“With weeping and with wailing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou spirit maledict, do thou remain;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For thee I know, though thou art all defiled.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Then stretched he both his hands unto the boat;
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Whereat my wary Master thrust him back,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Saying, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Away there with the other dogs!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Thereafter with his arms he clasped my neck;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* He kissed my face, and said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Disdainful soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Blessed be she who bore thee in her bosom.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That was an arrogant person in the world;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Goodness is none, that decks his memory;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So likewise here his shade is furious.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*How many are esteemed great kings up there,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who here shall be like unto swine in mire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Leaving behind them horrible dispraises!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, much should I be pleased,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If I could see him soused into this broth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Before we issue forth out of the lake.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ere unto thee the shore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Reveal itself, thou shalt be satisfied;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Such a desire ’tis meet thou shouldst enjoy.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* A little after that, I saw such havoc
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Made of him by the people of the mire,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* That still I praise and thank my God for it.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* They all were shouting, \zsay-s |who="The souls"\*“At Philippo Argenti!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* And that exasperate spirit Florentine
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Turned round upon himself with his own teeth.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* We left him there, and more of him I tell not;
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* But on mine ears there smote a lamentation,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Whence forward I intent unbar mine eyes.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And the good Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Even now, my Son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The city draweth near whose name is Dis,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With the grave citizens, with the great throng.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Its mosques already, Master, clearly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Within there in the valley I discern\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Vermilion, as if issuing from the fire\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*They were.”\zsay-e\* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The fire eternal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That kindles them within makes them look red,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As thou beholdest in this nether Hell.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Then we arrived within the moats profound,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* That circumvallate that disconsolate city;
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* The walls appeared to me to be of iron.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Not without making first a circuit wide,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* We came unto a place where loud the pilot
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Cried out to us, \zsay-s |who="Phlegyas"\*“Debark, here is the entrance.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* More than a thousand at the gates I saw
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Out of the Heavens rained down, who angrily
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Were saying, \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*“Who is this that without death\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*Goes through the kingdom of the people dead?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* And my sagacious Master made a sign
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Of wishing secretly to speak with them.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* A little then they quelled their great disdain,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*“Come thou alone, and he begone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*Who has so boldly entered these dominions.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*Let him return alone by his mad road;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*Try, if he can; for thou shalt here remain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The demons"\*Who hast escorted him through such dark regions.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Think, Reader, if I was discomforted
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* At utterance of the accursed words;
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* For never to return here I believed.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my dear Guide, who more than seven times\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Hast rendered me security, and drawn me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From imminent peril that before me stood,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Do not desert me,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thus undone;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if the going farther be denied us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Let us retrace our steps together swiftly.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And that Lord, who had led me thitherward,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Fear not; because our passage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*None can take from us, it by Such is given.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But here await me, and thy weary spirit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Comfort and nourish with a better hope;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For in this nether world I will not leave thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* So onward goes and there abandons me
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* My Father sweet, and I remain in doubt,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* For No and Yes within my head contend.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* I could not hear what he proposed to them;
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* But with them there he did not linger long,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Ere each within in rivalry ran back.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* They closed the portals, those our adversaries,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* On my Lord’s breast, who had remained without
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* And turned to me with footsteps far between.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* His eyes cast down, his forehead shorn had he
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Of all its boldness, and he said, with sighs,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Who has denied to me the dolesome houses?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And unto me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou, because I am angry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fear not, for I will conquer in the trial,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whatever for defence within be planned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This arrogance of theirs is nothing new;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For once they used it at less secret gate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which finds itself without a fastening still.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*O’er it didst thou behold the dead inscription;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And now this side of it descends the steep,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Passing across the circles without escort,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*One by whose means the city shall be opened.”\zsay-e\*
\c 9
\cp 9
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* That hue which cowardice brought out on me,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Beholding my Conductor backward turn,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Sooner repressed within him his new colour.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* He stopped attentive, like a man who listens,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Because the eye could not conduct him far
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Through the black air, and through the heavy fog.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Still it behoveth us to win the fight,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Began he; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Else. . .Such offered us herself. . .\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*O how I long that some one here arrive!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Well I perceived, as soon as the beginning
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* He covered up with what came afterward,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* That they were words quite different from the first;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* But none the less his saying gave me fear,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Because I carried out the broken phrase,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Perhaps to a worse meaning than he had.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Into this bottom of the doleful conch\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Doth any e’er descend from the first grade,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which for its pain has only hope cut off?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* This question put I; and he answered me:
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Seldom it comes to pass that one of us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Maketh the journey upon which I go.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*True is it, once before I here below\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was conjured by that pitiless Erictho,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who summoned back the shades unto their bodies.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Naked of me short while the flesh had been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Before within that wall she made me enter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To bring a spirit from the circle of Judas;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That is the lowest region and the darkest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And farthest from the heaven which circles all.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Well know I the way; therefore be reassured.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This fen, which a prodigious stench exhales,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Encompasses about the city dolent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where now we cannot enter without anger.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And more he said, but not in mind I have it;
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Because mine eye had altogether drawn me
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Tow’rds the high tower with the red-flaming summit,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Where in a moment saw I swift uprisen
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* The three infernal Furies stained with blood,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Who had the limbs of women and their mien,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* And with the greenest hydras were begirt;
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Small serpents and cerastes were their tresses,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Wherewith their horrid temples were entwined.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And he who well the handmaids of the Queen
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Of everlasting lamentation knew,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Behold the fierce Erinnys.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This is Megaera, on the left-hand side;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She who is weeping on the right, Alecto;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Tisiphone is between;”\zsay-e\* and then was silent.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Each one her breast was rending with her nails;
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* They beat them with their palms, and cried so loud,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* That I for dread pressed close unto the Poet.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Furies"\*“Medusa come, so we to stone will change him!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* All shouted looking down; \zsay-s |who="The Furies"\*“in evil hour\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Furies"\*Avenged we not on Theseus his assault!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Turn thyself round, and keep thine eyes close shut,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For if the Gorgon appear, and thou shouldst see it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No more returning upward would there be.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Thus said the Master; and he turned me round
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Himself, and trusted not unto my hands
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* So far as not to blind me with his own.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* O ye who have undistempered intellects,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Observe the doctrine that conceals itself
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Beneath the veil of the mysterious verses!
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And now there came across the turbid waves
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* The clangour of a sound with terror fraught,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Because of which both of the margins trembled;
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Not otherwise it was than of a wind
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Impetuous on account of adverse heats,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* That smites the forest, and, without restraint,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* The branches rends, beats down, and bears away;
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Right onward, laden with dust, it goes superb,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And puts to flight the wild beasts and the shepherds.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Mine eyes he loosed, and said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Direct the nerve\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of vision now along that ancient foam,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There yonder where that smoke is most intense.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Even as the frogs before the hostile serpent
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Across the water scatter all abroad,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Until each one is huddled in the earth.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* More than a thousand ruined souls I saw,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Thus fleeing from before one who on foot
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Was passing o’er the Styx with soles unwet.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* From off his face he fanned that unctuous air,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Waving his left hand oft in front of him,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* And only with that anguish seemed he weary.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Well I perceived one sent from Heaven was he,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* And to the Master turned; and he made sign
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* That I should quiet stand, and bow before him.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Ah! how disdainful he appeared to me!
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* He reached the gate, and with a little rod
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* He opened it, for there was no resistance.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“O banished out of Heaven, people despised!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Thus he began upon the horrid threshold;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*“Whence is this arrogance within you couched?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Wherefore recalcitrate against that will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*From which the end can never be cut off,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*And which has many times increased your pain?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*What helpeth it to butt against the fates?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*Your Cerberus, if you remember well,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="The angel"\*For that still bears his chin and gullet peeled.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Then he returned along the miry road,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* And spake no word to us, but had the look
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Of one whom other care constrains and goads
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Than that of him who in his presence is;
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And we our feet directed tow’rds the city,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* After those holy words all confident.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Within we entered without any contest;
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* And I, who inclination had to see
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* What the condition such a fortress holds,
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Soon as I was within, cast round mine eye,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* And see on every hand an ample plain,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Full of distress and torment terrible.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Even as at Arles, where stagnant grows the Rhone,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Even as at Pola near to the Quarnaro,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* That shuts in Italy and bathes its borders,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* The sepulchres make all the place uneven;
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* So likewise did they there on every side,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Saving that there the manner was more bitter;
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* For flames between the sepulchres were scattered,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* By which they so intensely heated were,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* That iron more so asks not any art.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* All of their coverings uplifted were,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* And from them issued forth such dire laments,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Sooth seemed they of the wretched and tormented.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, what are all those people\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who, having sepulture within those tombs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make themselves audible by doleful sighs?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Here are the Heresiarchs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With their disciples of all sects, and much\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*More than thou thinkest laden are the tombs.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here like together with its like is buried;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And more and less the monuments are heated.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* And when he to the right had turned, we passed
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Between the torments and high parapets.
\c 10
\cp 10
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Now onward goes, along a narrow path
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Between the torments and the city wall,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* My Master, and I follow at his back.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O power supreme, that through these impious circles\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Turnest me,”\zsay-e\* I began, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“as pleases thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Speak to me, and my longings satisfy;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The people who are lying in these tombs,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Might they be seen? already are uplifted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The covers all, and no one keepeth guard.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“They all will be closed up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When from Jehoshaphat they shall return\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here with the bodies they have left above.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Their cemetery have upon this side\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With Epicurus all his followers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who with the body mortal make the soul;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But in the question thou dost put to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Within here shalt thou soon be satisfied,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And likewise in the wish thou keepest silent.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Good Leader, I but keep concealed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From thee my heart, that I may speak the less,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Nor only now hast thou thereto disposed me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“O Tuscan, thou who through the city of fire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Goest alive, thus speaking modestly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Be pleased to stay thy footsteps in this place.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Thy mode of speaking makes thee manifest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*A native of that noble fatherland,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*To which perhaps I too molestful was.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Upon a sudden issued forth this sound
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* From out one of the tombs; wherefore I pressed,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Fearing, a little nearer to my Leader.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And unto me he said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Turn thee; what dost thou?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold there Farinata who has risen;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From the waist upwards wholly shalt thou see him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* I had already fixed mine eyes on his,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* And he uprose erect with breast and front
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* E’en as if Hell he had in great despite.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And with courageous hands and prompt my Leader
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Thrust me between the sepulchres towards him,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Exclaiming, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let thy words explicit be.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* As soon as I was at the foot of his tomb
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Somewhat he eyed me, and, as if disdainful,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Then asked of me, \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“Who were thine ancestors?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* I, who desirous of obeying was,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Concealed it not, but all revealed to him;
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Whereat he raised his brows a little upward.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Then said he: \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“Fiercely adverse have they been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*To me, and to my fathers, and my party;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*So that two several times I scattered them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If they were banished, they returned on all sides,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* I answered him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“the first time and the second;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But yours have not acquired that art aright.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Then there uprose upon the sight, uncovered
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Down to the chin, a shadow at his side;
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* I think that he had risen on his knees.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Round me he gazed, as if solicitude
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* He had to see if some one else were with me,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* But after his suspicion was all spent,
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Weeping, he said to me: \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*“If through this blind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*Prison thou goest by loftiness of genius,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*Where is my son? and why is he not with thee?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I come not of myself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*He who is waiting yonder leads me here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whom in disdain perhaps your Guido had.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* His language and the mode of punishment
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Already unto me had read his name;
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* On that account my answer was so full.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Up starting suddenly, he cried out: \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*“How\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*Saidst thou,—he had? Is he not still alive?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cavalcante"\*Does not the sweet light strike upon his eyes?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* When he became aware of some delay,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Which I before my answer made, supine
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* He fell again, and forth appeared no more.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* But the other, magnanimous, at whose desire
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* I had remained, did not his aspect change,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Neither his neck he moved, nor bent his side.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“And if,”\zsay-e\* continuing his first discourse,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“They have that art,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“not learned aright,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*That more tormenteth me, than doth this bed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*But fifty times shall not rekindled be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*The countenance of the Lady who reigns here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Ere thou shalt know how heavy is that art;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*And as thou wouldst to the sweet world return,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Say why that people is so pitiless\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Against my race in each one of its laws?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Whence I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The slaughter and great carnage\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which have with crimson stained the Arbia, cause\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Such orisons in our temple to be made.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* After his head he with a sigh had shaken,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“There I was not alone,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“nor surely\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Without a cause had with the others moved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*But there I was alone, where every one\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Consented to the laying waste of Florence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*He who defended her with open face.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ah! so hereafter may your seed repose,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* I him entreated, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“solve for me that knot,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which has entangled my conceptions here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*It seems that you can see, if I hear rightly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Beforehand whatsoe’er time brings with it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And in the present have another mode.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“We see, like those who have imperfect sight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*The things,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“that distant are from us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*So much still shines on us the Sovereign Ruler.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*When they draw near, or are, is wholly vain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Our intellect, and if none brings it to us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Not anything know we of your human state.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Hence thou canst understand, that wholly dead\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Will be our knowledge from the moment when\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*The portal of the future shall be closed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Then I, as if compunctious for my fault,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Now, then, you will tell that fallen one,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That still his son is with the living joined.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if just now, in answering, I was dumb,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell him I did it because I was thinking\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Already of the error you have solved me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And now my Master was recalling me,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Wherefore more eagerly I prayed the spirit
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* That he would tell me who was with him there.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* He said: \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*“With more than a thousand here I lie;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*Within here is the second Frederick,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Farinata"\*And the Cardinal, and of the rest I speak not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Thereon he hid himself; and I towards
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* The ancient poet turned my steps, reflecting
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Upon that saying, which seemed hostile to me.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* He moved along; and afterward thus going,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* He said to me, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Why art thou so bewildered?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* And I in his inquiry satisfied him.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Let memory preserve what thou hast heard\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Against thyself,”\zsay-e\* that Sage commanded me,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“And now attend here;”\zsay-e\* and he raised his finger.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“When thou shalt be before the radiance sweet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of her whose beauteous eyes all things behold,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From her thou’lt know the journey of thy life.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Unto the left hand then he turned his feet;
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* We left the wall, and went towards the middle,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Along a path that strikes into a valley,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Which even up there unpleasant made its stench.
\c 11
\cp 11
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Upon the margin of a lofty bank
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Which great rocks broken in a circle made,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* We came upon a still more cruel throng;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* And there, by reason of the horrible
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Excess of stench the deep abyss throws out,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* We drew ourselves aside behind the cover
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Of a great tomb, whereon I saw a writing,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Which said: \zsay-s |who="The tomb"\*“Pope Anastasius I hold,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="The tomb"\*Whom out of the right way Photinus drew.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Slow it behoveth our descent to be,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that the sense be first a little used\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To the sad blast, and then we shall not heed it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* The Master thus; and unto him I said,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Some compensation find, that the time pass not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Idly;”\zsay-e\* and he: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou seest I think of that.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*My son, upon the inside of these rocks,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Began he then to say, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“are three small circles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From grade to grade, like those which thou art leaving.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They all are full of spirits maledict;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But that hereafter sight alone suffice thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hear how and wherefore they are in constraint.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of every malice that wins hate in Heaven,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Injury is the end; and all such end\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Either by force or fraud afflicteth others.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But because fraud is man’s peculiar vice,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*More it displeases God; and so stand lowest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The fraudulent, and greater dole assails them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*All the first circle of the Violent is;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But since force may be used against three persons,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In three rounds ’tis divided and constructed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To God, to ourselves, and to our neighbour can we\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Use force; I say on them and on their things,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As thou shalt hear with reason manifest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A death by violence, and painful wounds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Are to our neighbour given; and in his substance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ruin, and arson, and injurious levies;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whence homicides, and he who smites unjustly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Marauders, and freebooters, the first round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Tormenteth all in companies diverse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Man may lay violent hands upon himself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And his own goods; and therefore in the second\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Round must perforce without avail repent\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whoever of your world deprives himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who games, and dissipates his property,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And weepeth there, where he should jocund be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Violence can be done the Deity,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In heart denying and blaspheming Him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And by disdaining Nature and her bounty.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And for this reason doth the smallest round\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Seal with its signet Sodom and Cahors,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And who, disdaining God, speaks from the heart.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fraud, wherewithal is every conscience stung,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A man may practise upon him who trusts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And him who doth no confidence imburse.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This latter mode, it would appear, dissevers\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Only the bond of love which Nature makes;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Wherefore within the second circle nestle\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hypocrisy, flattery, and who deals in magic,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Falsification, theft, and simony,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Panders, and barrators, and the like filth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By the other mode, forgotten is that love\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which Nature makes, and what is after added,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From which there is a special faith engendered.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hence in the smallest circle, where the point is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the Universe, upon which Dis is seated,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whoe’er betrays for ever is consumed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, clear enough proceeds\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy reasoning, and full well distinguishes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This cavern and the people who possess it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me, those within the fat lagoon,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Whom the wind drives, and whom the rain doth beat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And who encounter with such bitter tongues,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Wherefore are they inside of the red city\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Not punished, if God has them in his wrath,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if he has not, wherefore in such fashion?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And unto me he said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Why wanders so\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thine intellect from that which it is wont?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or, sooth, thy mind where is it elsewhere looking?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Hast thou no recollection of those words\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With which thine Ethics thoroughly discusses\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The dispositions three, that Heaven abides not,—\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Incontinence, and Malice, and insane\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bestiality? and how Incontinence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Less God offendeth, and less blame attracts?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If thou regardest this conclusion well,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And to thy mind recallest who they are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That up outside are undergoing penance,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Clearly wilt thou perceive why from these felons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They separated are, and why less wroth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Justice divine doth smite them with its hammer.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Sun, that healest all distempered vision,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou dost content me so, when thou resolvest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That doubting pleases me no less than knowing!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Once more a little backward turn thee,”\zsay-e\* said I,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“There where thou sayest that usury offends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Goodness divine, and disengage the knot.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Philosophy,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“to him who heeds it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Noteth, not only in one place alone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After what manner Nature takes her course\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From Intellect Divine, and from its art;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if thy Physics carefully thou notest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After not many pages shalt thou find,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That this your art as far as possible\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Follows, as the disciple doth the master;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that your art is, as it were, God’s grandchild.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From these two, if thou bringest to thy mind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Genesis at the beginning, it behoves\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Mankind to gain their life and to advance;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And since the usurer takes another way,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nature herself and in her follower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Disdains he, for elsewhere he puts his hope.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But follow, now, as I would fain go on,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For quivering are the Fishes on the horizon,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And the Wain wholly over Caurus lies,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And far beyond there we descend the crag.”\zsay-e\*
\c 12
\cp 12
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The place where to descend the bank we came
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Was alpine, and from what was there, moreover,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Of such a kind that every eye would shun it.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Such as that ruin is which in the flank
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Smote, on this side of Trent, the Adige,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Either by earthquake or by failing stay,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* For from the mountain’s top, from which it moved,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Unto the plain the cliff is shattered so,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Some path ’twould give to him who was above;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Even such was the descent of that ravine,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* And on the border of the broken chasm
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* The infamy of Crete was stretched along,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Who was conceived in the fictitious cow;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And when he us beheld, he bit himself,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Even as one whom anger racks within.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* My Sage towards him shouted: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou think’st that here may be the Duke of Athens,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who in the world above brought death to thee?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Get thee gone, beast, for this one cometh not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Instructed by thy sister, but he comes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In order to behold your punishments.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As is that bull who breaks loose at the moment
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* In which he has received the mortal blow,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Who cannot walk, but staggers here and there,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* The Minotaur beheld I do the like;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And he, the wary, cried: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Run to the passage;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*While he is wroth, ’tis well thou shouldst descend.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Thus down we took our way o’er that discharge
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Of stones, which oftentimes did move themselves
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Beneath my feet, from the unwonted burden.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Thoughtful I went; and he said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou art thinking\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Perhaps upon this ruin, which is guarded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By that brute anger which just now I quenched.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now will I have thee know, the other time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*I here descended to the nether Hell,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This precipice had not yet fallen down.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But truly, if I well discern, a little\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Before His coming who the mighty spoil\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Bore off from Dis, in the supernal circle,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon all sides the deep and loathsome valley\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Trembled so, that I thought the Universe\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was thrilled with love, by which there are who think\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The world ofttimes converted into chaos;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And at that moment this primeval crag\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Both here and elsewhere made such overthrow.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But fix thine eyes below; for draweth near\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The river of blood, within which boiling is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whoe’er by violence doth injure others.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* O blind cupidity, O wrath insane,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* That spurs us onward so in our short life,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* And in the eternal then so badly steeps us!
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* I saw an ample moat bent like a bow,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* As one which all the plain encompasses,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Conformable to what my Guide had said.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And between this and the embankment’s foot
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Centaurs in file were running, armed with arrows,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* As in the world they used the chase to follow.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Beholding us descend, each one stood still,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* And from the squadron three detached themselves,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* With bows and arrows in advance selected;
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And from afar one cried: \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*“Unto what torment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Come ye, who down the hillside are descending?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Tell us from there; if not, I draw the bow.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* My Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Our answer will we make\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To Chiron, near you there; in evil hour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That will of thine was evermore so hasty.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Then touched he me, and said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This one is Nessus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who perished for the lovely Dejanira,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And for himself, himself did vengeance take.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And he in the midst, who at his breast is gazing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Is the great Chiron, who brought up Achilles;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That other Pholus is, who was so wrathful.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thousands and thousands go about the moat\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Shooting with shafts whatever soul emerges\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Out of the blood, more than his crime allots.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Near we approached unto those monsters fleet;
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Chiron an arrow took, and with the notch
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Backward upon his jaws he put his beard.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* After he had uncovered his great mouth,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* He said to his companions: \zsay-s |who="Chiron"\*“Are you ware\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Chiron"\*That he behind moveth whate’er he touches?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Chiron"\*Thus are not wont to do the feet of dead men.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* And my good Guide, who now was at his breast,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Where the two natures are together joined,
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Replied: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Indeed he lives, and thus alone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Me it behoves to show him the dark valley;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Necessity, and not delight, impels us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Some one withdrew from singing Halleluja,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who unto me committed this new office;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No thief is he, nor I a thievish spirit.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But by that virtue through which I am moving\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*My steps along this savage thoroughfare,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Give us some one of thine, to be with us,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And who may show us where to pass the ford,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And who may carry this one on his back;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For ’tis no spirit that can walk the air.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Upon his right breast Chiron wheeled about,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* And said to Nessus: \zsay-s |who="Chiron"\*“Turn and do thou guide them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Chiron"\*And warn aside, if other band may meet you.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* We with our faithful escort onward moved
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Along the brink of the vermilion boiling,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Wherein the boiled were uttering loud laments.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* People I saw within up to the eyebrows,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And the great Centaur said: \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*“Tyrants are these,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Who dealt in bloodshed and in pillaging.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Here they lament their pitiless mischiefs; here\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Is Alexander, and fierce Dionysius\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Who upon Sicily brought dolorous years.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*That forehead there which has the hair so black\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Is Azzolin; and the other who is blond,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Obizzo is of Esti, who, in truth,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Up in the world was by his stepson slain.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Then turned I to the Poet; and he said,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now he be first to thee, and second I.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* A little farther on the Centaur stopped
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Above a folk, who far down as the throat
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Seemed from that boiling stream to issue forth.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* A shade he showed us on one side alone,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*“He cleft asunder in God’s bosom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*The heart that still upon the Thames is honoured.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Then people saw I, who from out the river
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Lifted their heads and also all the chest;
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* And many among these I recognised.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Thus ever more and more grew shallower
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* That blood, so that the feet alone it covered;
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* And there across the moat our passage was.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*“Even as thou here upon this side beholdest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*The boiling stream, that aye diminishes,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* The Centaur said, \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*“I wish thee to believe\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*That on this other more and more declines\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Its bed, until it reunites itself\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Where it behoveth tyranny to groan.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Justice divine, upon this side, is goading\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*That Attila, who was a scourge on earth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*And Pyrrhus, and Sextus; and for ever milks\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*The tears which with the boiling it unseals\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*In Rinier da Corneto and Rinier Pazzo,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nessus"\*Who made upon the highways so much war.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Then back he turned, and passed again the ford.
\c 13
\cp 13
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Not yet had Nessus reached the other side,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* When we had put ourselves within a wood,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That was not marked by any path whatever.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Not foliage green, but of a dusky colour,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Not branches smooth, but gnarled and intertangled,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Not apple-trees were there, but thorns with poison.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Such tangled thickets have not, nor so dense,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Those savage wild beasts, that in hatred hold
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* ’Twixt Cecina and Corneto the tilled places.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* There do the hideous Harpies make their nests,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Who chased the Trojans from the Strophades,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* With sad announcement of impending doom;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Broad wings have they, and necks and faces human,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And feet with claws, and their great bellies fledged;
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* They make laments upon the wondrous trees.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And the good Master: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Ere thou enter farther,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Know that thou art within the second round,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Thus he began to say, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“and shalt be, till\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou comest out upon the horrible sand;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore look well around, and thou shalt see\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Things that will credence give unto my speech.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* I heard on all sides lamentations uttered,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And person none beheld I who might make them,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Whence, utterly bewildered, I stood still.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I think he thought that I perhaps might think
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* So many voices issued through those trunks
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* From people who concealed themselves from us;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Therefore the Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If thou break off\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Some little spray from any of these trees,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The thoughts thou hast will wholly be made vain.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Then stretched I forth my hand a little forward,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And plucked a branchlet off from a great thorn;
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* And the trunk cried, \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*“Why dost thou mangle me?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* After it had become embrowned with blood,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* It recommenced its cry: \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*“Why dost thou rend me?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Hast thou no spirit of pity whatsoever?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Men once we were, and now are changed to trees;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Indeed, thy hand should be more pitiful,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Even if the souls of serpents we had been.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* As out of a green brand, that is on fire
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* At one of the ends, and from the other drips
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And hisses with the wind that is escaping;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* So from that splinter issued forth together
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Both words and blood; whereat I let the tip
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Fall, and stood like a man who is afraid.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Had he been able sooner to believe,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* My Sage made answer, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O thou wounded soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What only in my verses he has seen,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Not upon thee had he stretched forth his hand;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whereas the thing incredible has caused me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To put him to an act which grieveth me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But tell him who thou wast, so that by way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of some amends thy fame he may refresh\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Up in the world, to which he can return.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And the trunk said: \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*“So thy sweet words allure me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*I cannot silent be; and you be vexed not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*That I a little to discourse am tempted.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*I am the one who both keys had in keeping\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Of Frederick’s heart, and turned them to and fro\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*So softly in unlocking and in locking,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*That from his secrets most men I withheld;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Fidelity I bore the glorious office\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*So great, I lost thereby my sleep and pulses.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*The courtesan who never from the dwelling\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Of Caesar turned aside her strumpet eyes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Death universal and the vice of courts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Inflamed against me all the other minds,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*And they, inflamed, did so inflame Augustus,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*That my glad honours turned to dismal mournings.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*My spirit, in disdainful exultation,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Thinking by dying to escape disdain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Made me unjust against myself, the just.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*I, by the roots unwonted of this wood,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Do swear to you that never broke I faith\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Unto my lord, who was so worthy of honour;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*And to the world if one of you return,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Let him my memory comfort, which is lying\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Still prostrate from the blow that envy dealt it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Waited awhile, and then: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Since he is silent,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* The Poet said to me, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“lose not the time,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But speak, and question him, if more may please thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Whence I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Do thou again inquire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Concerning what thou think’st will satisfy me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For I cannot, such pity is in my heart.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Therefore he recommenced: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“So may the man\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do for thee freely what thy speech implores,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Spirit incarcerate, again be pleased\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To tell us in what way the soul is bound\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Within these knots; and tell us, if thou canst,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If any from such members e’er is freed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then blew the trunk amain, and afterward
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* The wind was into such a voice converted:
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*“With brevity shall be replied to you.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*When the exasperated soul abandons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*The body whence it rent itself away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Minos consigns it to the seventh abyss.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*It falls into the forest, and no part\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Is chosen for it; but where Fortune hurls it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*There like a grain of spelt it germinates.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*It springs a sapling, and a forest tree;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*The Harpies, feeding then upon its leaves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Do pain create, and for the pain an outlet.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Like others for our spoils shall we return;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*But not that any one may them revest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*For ’tis not just to have what one casts off.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Here we shall drag them, and along the dismal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Forest our bodies shall suspended be,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier della Vigna"\*Each to the thorn of his molested shade.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* We were attentive still unto the trunk,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Thinking that more it yet might wish to tell us,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* When by a tumult we were overtaken,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* In the same way as he is who perceives
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* The boar and chase approaching to his stand,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Who hears the crashing of the beasts and branches;
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And two behold! upon our left-hand side,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Naked and scratched, fleeing so furiously,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* That of the forest, every fan they broke.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* He who was in advance: \zsay-s |who="Lano"\*“Now help, Death, help!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* And the other one, who seemed to lag too much,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Was shouting: \zsay-s |who="Jacopo da Sant'Andrea"\*“Lano, were not so alert\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo da Sant'Andrea"\*Those legs of thine at joustings of the Toppo!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* And then, perchance because his breath was failing,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* He grouped himself together with a bush.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Behind them was the forest full of black
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* She-mastiffs, ravenous, and swift of foot
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* As greyhounds, who are issuing from the chain.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* On him who had crouched down they set their teeth,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* And him they lacerated piece by piece,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Thereafter bore away those aching members.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Thereat my Escort took me by the hand,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And led me to the bush, that all in vain
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Was weeping from its bloody lacerations.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*“O Jacopo,”\zsay-e\* it said, \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*“of Sant’ Andrea,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*What helped it thee of me to make a screen?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*What blame have I in thy nefarious life?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* When near him had the Master stayed his steps,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* He said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Who wast thou, that through wounds so many\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Art blowing out with blood thy dolorous speech?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* And he to us: \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*“O souls, that hither come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*To look upon the shameful massacre\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*That has so rent away from me my leaves,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Gather them up beneath the dismal bush;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*I of that city was which to the Baptist\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Changed its first patron, wherefore he for this\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Forever with his art will make it sad.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*And were it not that on the pass of Arno\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Some glimpses of him are remaining still,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Those citizens, who afterwards rebuilt it\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Upon the ashes left by Attila,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*In vain had caused their labour to be done.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="A Florentine suicide"\*Of my own house I made myself a gibbet.”\zsay-e\*
\c 14
\cp 14
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Because the charity of my native place
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Constrained me, gathered I the scattered leaves,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And gave them back to him, who now was hoarse.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Then came we to the confine, where disparted
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* The second round is from the third, and where
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* A horrible form of Justice is beheld.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Clearly to manifest these novel things,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* I say that we arrived upon a plain,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Which from its bed rejecteth every plant;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* The dolorous forest is a garland to it
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* All round about, as the sad moat to that;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* There close upon the edge we stayed our feet.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* The soil was of an arid and thick sand,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Not of another fashion made than that
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Which by the feet of Cato once was pressed.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Vengeance of God, O how much oughtest thou
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* By each one to be dreaded, who doth read
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* That which was manifest unto mine eyes!
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Of naked souls beheld I many herds,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Who all were weeping very miserably,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And over them seemed set a law diverse.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Supine upon the ground some folk were lying;
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And some were sitting all drawn up together,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* And others went about continually.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Those who were going round were far the more,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* And those were less who lay down to their torment,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* But had their tongues more loosed to lamentation.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* O’er all the sand-waste, with a gradual fall,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Were raining down dilated flakes of fire,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* As of the snow on Alp without a wind.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* As Alexander, in those torrid parts
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Of India, beheld upon his host
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Flames fall unbroken till they reached the ground.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Whence he provided with his phalanxes
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* To trample down the soil, because the vapour
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Better extinguished was while it was single;
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Thus was descending the eternal heat,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Whereby the sand was set on fire, like tinder
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Beneath the steel, for doubling of the dole.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Without repose forever was the dance
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Of miserable hands, now there, now here,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Shaking away from off them the fresh gleeds.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master,”\zsay-e\* began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thou who overcomest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*All things except the demons dire, that issued\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Against us at the entrance of the gate,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who is that mighty one who seems to heed not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The fire, and lieth lowering and disdainful,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that the rain seems not to ripen him?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* And he himself, who had become aware
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* That I was questioning my Guide about him,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Cried: \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*“Such as I was living, am I, dead.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*If Jove should weary out his smith, from whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*He seized in anger the sharp thunderbolt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*Wherewith upon the last day I was smitten,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*And if he wearied out by turns the others\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*In Mongibello at the swarthy forge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*Vociferating, ‘Help, good Vulcan, help!’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*Even as he did there at the fight of Phlegra,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*And shot his bolts at me with all his might,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capaneus"\*He would not have thereby a joyous vengeance.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Then did my Leader speak with such great force,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* That I had never heard him speak so loud:
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O Capaneus, in that is not extinguished\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thine arrogance, thou punished art the more;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Not any torment, saving thine own rage,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Would be unto thy fury pain complete.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Then he turned round to me with better lip,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“One of the Seven Kings was he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who Thebes besieged, and held, and seems to hold\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*God in disdain, and little seems to prize him;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But, as I said to him, his own despites\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Are for his breast the fittest ornaments.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now follow me, and mind thou do not place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As yet thy feet upon the burning sand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But always keep them close unto the wood.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Speaking no word, we came to where there gushes
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Forth from the wood a little rivulet,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Whose redness makes my hair still stand on end.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* As from the Bulicame springs the brooklet,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* The sinful women later share among them,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* So downward through the sand it went its way.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* The bottom of it, and both sloping banks,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Were made of stone, and the margins at the side;
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Whence I perceived that there the passage was.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“In all the rest which I have shown to thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Since we have entered in within the gate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whose threshold unto no one is denied,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Nothing has been discovered by thine eyes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So notable as is the present river,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which all the little flames above it quenches.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* These words were of my Leader; whence I prayed him
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* That he would give me largess of the food,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* For which he had given me largess of desire.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“In the mid-sea there sits a wasted land,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Said he thereafterward, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“whose name is Crete,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Under whose king the world of old was chaste.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There is a mountain there, that once was glad\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With waters and with leaves, which was called Ida;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now ’tis deserted, as a thing worn out.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Rhea once chose it for the faithful cradle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of her own son; and to conceal him better,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whene’er he cried, she there had clamours made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A grand old man stands in the mount erect,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who holds his shoulders turned tow’rds Damietta,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And looks at Rome as if it were his mirror.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*His head is fashioned of refined gold,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And of pure silver are the arms and breast;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then he is brass as far down as the fork.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From that point downward all is chosen iron,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Save that the right foot is of kiln-baked clay,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And more he stands on that than on the other.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Each part, except the gold, is by a fissure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Asunder cleft, that dripping is with tears,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which gathered together perforate that cavern.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From rock to rock they fall into this valley;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Acheron, Styx, and Phlegethon they form;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Then downward go along this narrow sluice\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Unto that point where is no more descending.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They form Cocytus; what that pool may be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou shalt behold, so here ’tis not narrated.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If so the present runnel\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Doth take its rise in this way from our world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Why only on this verge appears it to us?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou knowest the place is round,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And notwithstanding thou hast journeyed far,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still to the left descending to the bottom,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou hast not yet through all the circle turned.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore if something new appear to us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It should not bring amazement to thy face.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And I again: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, where shall be found\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Lethe and Phlegethon, for of one thou’rt silent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And sayest the other of this rain is made?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“In all thy questions truly thou dost please me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Replied he; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“but the boiling of the red\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Water might well solve one of them thou makest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou shalt see Lethe, but outside this moat,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There where the souls repair to lave themselves,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When sin repented of has been removed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Then said he: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“It is time now to abandon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The wood; take heed that thou come after me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A way the margins make that are not burning,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And over them all vapours are extinguished.”\zsay-e\*
\c 15
\cp 15
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Now bears us onward one of the hard margins,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* And so the brooklet’s mist o’ershadows it,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* From fire it saves the water and the dikes.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Even as the Flemings, ’twixt Cadsand and Bruges,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Fearing the flood that tow’rds them hurls itself,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Their bulwarks build to put the sea to flight;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And as the Paduans along the Brenta,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* To guard their villas and their villages,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Or ever Chiarentana feel the heat;
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* In such similitude had those been made,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Albeit not so lofty nor so thick,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Whoever he might be, the master made them.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Now were we from the forest so remote,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* I could not have discovered where it was,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Even if backward I had turned myself,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* When we a company of souls encountered,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Who came beside the dike, and every one
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Gazed at us, as at evening we are wont
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* To eye each other under a new moon,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* And so towards us sharpened they their brows
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* As an old tailor at the needle’s eye.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Thus scrutinised by such a family,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* By some one I was recognised, who seized
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* My garment’s hem, and cried out, \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“What a marvel!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And I, when he stretched forth his arm to me,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* On his baked aspect fastened so mine eyes,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That the scorched countenance prevented not
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* His recognition by my intellect;
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And bowing down my face unto his own,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* I made reply, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Are you here, Ser Brunetto?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“May’t not displease thee, O my son,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*If a brief space with thee Brunetto Latini\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Backward return and let the trail go on.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* I said to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“With all my power I ask it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And if you wish me to sit down with you,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I will, if he please, for I go with him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“O son,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“whoever of this herd\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*A moment stops, lies then a hundred years,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Nor fans himself when smiteth him the fire.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Therefore go on; I at thy skirts will come,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And afterward will I rejoin my band,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Which goes lamenting its eternal doom.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* I did not dare to go down from the road
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Level to walk with him; but my head bowed
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* I held as one who goeth reverently.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And he began: \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“What fortune or what fate\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Before the last day leadeth thee down here?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And who is this that showeth thee the way?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Up there above us in the life serene,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* I answered him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I lost me in a valley,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or ever yet my age had been completed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But yestermorn I turned my back upon it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This one appeared to me, returning thither,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And homeward leadeth me along this road.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“If thou thy star do follow,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Thou canst not fail thee of a glorious port,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*If well I judged in the life beautiful.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And if I had not died so prematurely,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Seeing Heaven thus benignant unto thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*I would have given thee comfort in the work.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*But that ungrateful and malignant people,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Which of old time from Fesole descended,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And smacks still of the mountain and the granite,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Will make itself, for thy good deeds, thy foe;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And it is right; for among crabbed sorbs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*It ill befits the sweet fig to bear fruit.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Old rumour in the world proclaims them blind;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*A people avaricious, envious, proud;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Take heed that of their customs thou do cleanse thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Thy fortune so much honour doth reserve thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*One party and the other shall be hungry\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*For thee; but far from goat shall be the grass.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Their litter let the beasts of Fesole\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Make of themselves, nor let them touch the plant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*If any still upon their dunghill rise,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*In which may yet revive the consecrated\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Seed of those Romans, who remained there when\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*The nest of such great malice it became.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If my entreaty wholly were fulfilled,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Replied I to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“not yet would you be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In banishment from human nature placed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For in my mind is fixed, and touches now\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My heart the dear and good paternal image\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Of you, when in the world from hour to hour\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*You taught me how a man becomes eternal;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And how much I am grateful, while I live\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Behoves that in my language be discerned.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*What you narrate of my career I write,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And keep it to be glossed with other text\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*By a Lady who can do it, if I reach her.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*This much will I have manifest to you;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Provided that my conscience do not chide me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For whatsoever Fortune I am ready.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Such handsel is not new unto mine ears;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore let Fortune turn her wheel around\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As it may please her, and the churl his mattock.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* My Master thereupon on his right cheek
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Did backward turn himself, and looked at me;
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Then said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“He listeneth well who noteth it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Nor speaking less on that account, I go
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* With Ser Brunetto, and I ask who are
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* His most known and most eminent companions.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*“To know of some is well;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Of others it were laudable to be silent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*For short would be the time for so much speech.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Know then, in sum, that all of them were clerks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And men of letters great and of great fame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*In the world tainted with the selfsame sin.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Priscian goes yonder with that wretched crowd,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*And Francis of Accorso; and thou hadst seen there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*If thou hadst had a hankering for such scurf,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*That one, who by the Servant of the Servants\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*From Arno was transferred to Bacchiglione,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Where he has left his sin-excited nerves.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*More would I say, but coming and discoursing\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Can be no longer; for that I behold\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*New smoke uprising yonder from the sand.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*A people comes with whom I may not be;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*Commended unto thee be my Tesoro,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Brunetto Latini"\*In which I still live, and no more I ask.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Then he turned round, and seemed to be of those
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Who at Verona run for the Green Mantle
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Across the plain; and seemed to be among them
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* The one who wins, and not the one who loses.
\c 16
\cp 16
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Now was I where was heard the reverberation
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of water falling into the next round,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Like to that humming which the beehives make,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* When shadows three together started forth,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Running, from out a company that passed
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Beneath the rain of the sharp martyrdom.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Towards us came they, and each one cried out:
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*“Stop, thou; for by thy garb to us thou seemest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*To be some one of our depraved city.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Ah me! what wounds I saw upon their limbs,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Recent and ancient by the flames burnt in!
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* It pains me still but to remember it.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Unto their cries my Teacher paused attentive;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* He turned his face towards me, and \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now wait,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* He said; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“to these we should be courteous.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And if it were not for the fire that darts\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The nature of this region, I should say\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That haste were more becoming thee than them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As soon as we stood still, they recommenced
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* The old refrain, and when they overtook us,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Formed of themselves a wheel, all three of them.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* As champions stripped and oiled are wont to do,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Watching for their advantage and their hold,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Before they come to blows and thrusts between them,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Thus, wheeling round, did every one his visage
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Direct to me, so that in opposite wise
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* His neck and feet continual journey made.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* And, \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*“If the misery of this soft place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Bring in disdain ourselves and our entreaties,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Began one, \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*“and our aspect black and blistered,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Let the renown of us thy mind incline\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*To tell us who thou art, who thus securely\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Thy living feet dost move along through Hell.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*He in whose footprints thou dost see me treading,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Naked and skinless though he now may go,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Was of a greater rank than thou dost think;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*He was the grandson of the good Gualdrada;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*His name was Guidoguerra, and in life\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Much did he with his wisdom and his sword.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*The other, who close by me treads the sand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Tegghiaio Aldobrandi is, whose fame\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Above there in the world should welcome be.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*And I, who with them on the cross am placed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Jacopo Rusticucci was; and truly\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*My savage wife, more than aught else, doth harm me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Could I have been protected from the fire,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Below I should have thrown myself among them,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* And think the Teacher would have suffered it;
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* But as I should have burned and baked myself,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* My terror overmastered my good will,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Which made me greedy of embracing them.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Then I began: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Sorrow and not disdain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Did your condition fix within me so,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That tardily it wholly is stripped off,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As soon as this my Lord said unto me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Words, on account of which I thought within me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That people such as you are were approaching.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I of your city am; and evermore\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Your labours and your honourable names\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I with affection have retraced and heard.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I leave the gall, and go for the sweet fruits\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Promised to me by the veracious Leader;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But to the centre first I needs must plunge.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*“So may the soul for a long while conduct\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Those limbs of thine,”\zsay-e\* did he make answer then,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*“And so may thy renown shine after thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Valour and courtesy, say if they dwell\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Within our city, as they used to do,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Or if they wholly have gone out of it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*For Guglielmo Borsier, who is in torment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*With us of late, and goes there with his comrades,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Jacopo Rusticucci"\*Doth greatly mortify us with his words.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“The new inhabitants and the sudden gains,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Pride and extravagance have in thee engendered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Florence, so that thou weep’st thereat already!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* In this wise I exclaimed with face uplifted;
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* And the three, taking that for my reply,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Looked at each other, as one looks at truth.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*“If other times so little it doth cost thee,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Replied they all, \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*“to satisfy another,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*Happy art thou, thus speaking at thy will!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*Therefore, if thou escape from these dark places,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*And come to rebehold the beauteous stars,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*When it shall pleasure thee to say, ‘I was,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="The three Florentines"\*See that thou speak of us unto the people.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Then they broke up the wheel, and in their flight
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* It seemed as if their agile legs were wings.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Not an Amen could possibly be said
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* So rapidly as they had disappeared;
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Wherefore the Master deemed best to depart.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* I followed him, and little had we gone,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Before the sound of water was so near us,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* That speaking we should hardly have been heard.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Even as that stream which holdeth its own course
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* The first from Monte Veso tow’rds the East,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Upon the left-hand slope of Apennine,
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Which is above called Acquacheta, ere
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* It down descendeth into its low bed,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* And at Forli is vacant of that name,
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Reverberates there above San Benedetto
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* From Alps, by falling at a single leap,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Where for a thousand there were room enough;
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Thus downward from a bank precipitate,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* We found resounding that dark-tinted water,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* So that it soon the ear would have offended.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* I had a cord around about me girt,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* And therewithal I whilom had designed
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* To take the panther with the painted skin.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* After I this had all from me unloosed,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* As my Conductor had commanded me,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* I reached it to him, gathered up and coiled,
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Whereat he turned himself to the right side,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And at a little distance from the verge,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* He cast it down into that deep abyss.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“It must needs be some novelty respond,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* I said within myself, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“to the new signal\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The Master with his eye is following so.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Ah me! how very cautious men should be
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* With those who not alone behold the act,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* But with their wisdom look into the thoughts!
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* He said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Soon there will upward come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What I await; and what thy thought is dreaming\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Must soon reveal itself unto thy sight.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Aye to that truth which has the face of falsehood,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* A man should close his lips as far as may be,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Because without his fault it causes shame;
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* But here I cannot; and, Reader, by the notes
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Of this my Comedy to thee I swear,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* So may they not be void of lasting favour,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Athwart that dense and darksome atmosphere
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* I saw a figure swimming upward come,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Marvellous unto every steadfast heart,
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Even as he returns who goeth down
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Sometimes to clear an anchor, which has grappled
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Reef, or aught else that in the sea is hidden,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Who upward stretches, and draws in his feet.
\c 17
\cp 17
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Behold the monster with the pointed tail,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who cleaves the hills, and breaketh walls and weapons,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold him who infecteth all the world.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Thus unto me my Guide began to say,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* And beckoned him that he should come to shore,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Near to the confine of the trodden marble;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And that uncleanly image of deceit
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Came up and thrust ashore its head and bust,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* But on the border did not drag its tail.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* The face was as the face of a just man,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Its semblance outwardly was so benign,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And of a serpent all the trunk beside.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Two paws it had, hairy unto the armpits;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The back, and breast, and both the sides it had
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Depicted o’er with nooses and with shields.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* With colours more, groundwork or broidery
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Never in cloth did Tartars make nor Turks,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Nor were such tissues by Arachne laid.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As sometimes wherries lie upon the shore,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* That part are in the water, part on land;
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And as among the guzzling Germans there,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* The beaver plants himself to wage his war;
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* So that vile monster lay upon the border,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Which is of stone, and shutteth in the sand.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* His tail was wholly quivering in the void,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Contorting upwards the envenomed fork,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That in the guise of scorpion armed its point.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The Guide said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now perforce must turn aside\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Our way a little, even to that beast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Malevolent, that yonder coucheth him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* We therefore on the right side descended,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And made ten steps upon the outer verge,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Completely to avoid the sand and flame;
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And after we are come to him, I see
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* A little farther off upon the sand
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* A people sitting near the hollow place.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Then said to me the Master: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“So that full\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Experience of this round thou bear away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now go and see what their condition is.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There let thy conversation be concise;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Till thou returnest I will speak with him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That he concede to us his stalwart shoulders.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Thus farther still upon the outermost
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Head of that seventh circle all alone
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* I went, where sat the melancholy folk.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Out of their eyes was gushing forth their woe;
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* This way, that way, they helped them with their hands
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Now from the flames and now from the hot soil.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Not otherwise in summer do the dogs,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Now with the foot, now with the muzzle, when
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* By fleas, or flies, or gadflies, they are bitten.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* When I had turned mine eyes upon the faces
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Of some, on whom the dolorous fire is falling,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Not one of them I knew; but I perceived
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* That from the neck of each there hung a pouch,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Which certain colour had, and certain blazon;
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* And thereupon it seems their eyes are feeding.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* And as I gazing round me come among them,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Upon a yellow pouch I azure saw
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* That had the face and posture of a lion.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Proceeding then the current of my sight,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Another of them saw I, red as blood,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Display a goose more white than butter is.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* And one, who with an azure sow and gravid
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Emblazoned had his little pouch of white,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*“What dost thou in this moat?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*Now get thee gone; and since thou’rt still alive,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*Know that a neighbour of mine, Vitaliano,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*Will have his seat here on my left-hand side.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*A Paduan am I with these Florentines;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*Full many a time they thunder in mine ears,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*Exclaiming, ‘Come the sovereign cavalier,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Reginaldo Scrovegni"\*He who shall bring the satchel with three goats;’”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Then twisted he his mouth, and forth he thrust
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* His tongue, like to an ox that licks its nose.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And fearing lest my longer stay might vex
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Him who had warned me not to tarry long,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Backward I turned me from those weary souls.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* I found my Guide, who had already mounted
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Upon the back of that wild animal,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* And said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now be both strong and bold.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Now we descend by stairways such as these;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Mount thou in front, for I will be midway,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that the tail may have no power to harm thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Such as he is who has so near the ague
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Of quartan that his nails are blue already,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* And trembles all, but looking at the shade;
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Even such became I at those proffered words;
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* But shame in me his menaces produced,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Which maketh servant strong before good master.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* I seated me upon those monstrous shoulders;
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* I wished to say, and yet the voice came not
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* As I believed, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Take heed that thou embrace me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* But he, who other times had rescued me
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* In other peril, soon as I had mounted,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Within his arms encircled and sustained me,
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now, Geryon, bestir thyself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The circles large, and the descent be little;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Think of the novel burden which thou hast.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Even as the little vessel shoves from shore,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Backward, still backward, so he thence withdrew;
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* And when he wholly felt himself afloat,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* There where his breast had been he turned his tail,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And that extended like an eel he moved,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* And with his paws drew to himself the air.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* A greater fear I do not think there was
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* What time abandoned Phaeton the reins,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* Whereby the heavens, as still appears, were scorched;
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Nor when the wretched Icarus his flanks
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Felt stripped of feathers by the melting wax,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* His father crying, \zsay-s |who="Daedalus"\*“An ill way thou takest!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Than was my own, when I perceived myself
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* On all sides in the air, and saw extinguished
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* The sight of everything but of the monster.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Onward he goeth, swimming slowly, slowly;
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Wheels and descends, but I perceive it only
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* By wind upon my face and from below.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* I heard already on the right the whirlpool
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Making a horrible crashing under us;
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* Whence I thrust out my head with eyes cast downward.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* Then was I still more fearful of the abyss;
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Because I fires beheld, and heard laments,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Whereat I, trembling, all the closer cling.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* I saw then, for before I had not seen it,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* The turning and descending, by great horrors
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* That were approaching upon divers sides.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* As falcon who has long been on the wing,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Who, without seeing either lure or bird,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Maketh the falconer say, \zsay-s |who="The falconer"\*“Ah me, thou stoopest,”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Descendeth weary, whence he started swiftly,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Thorough a hundred circles, and alights
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Far from his master, sullen and disdainful;
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Even thus did Geryon place us on the bottom,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Close to the bases of the rough-hewn rock,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* And being disencumbered of our persons,
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* He sped away as arrow from the string.
\c 18
\cp 18
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* There is a place in Hell called Malebolge,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Wholly of stone and of an iron colour,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As is the circle that around it turns.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Right in the middle of the field malign
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* There yawns a well exceeding wide and deep,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Of which its place the structure will recount.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Round, then, is that enclosure which remains
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Between the well and foot of the high, hard bank,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And has distinct in valleys ten its bottom.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* As where for the protection of the walls
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Many and many moats surround the castles,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* The part in which they are a figure forms,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Just such an image those presented there;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And as about such strongholds from their gates
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Unto the outer bank are little bridges,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* So from the precipice’s base did crags
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Project, which intersected dikes and moats,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Unto the well that truncates and collects them.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Within this place, down shaken from the back
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Of Geryon, we found us; and the Poet
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Held to the left, and I moved on behind.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Upon my right hand I beheld new anguish,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* New torments, and new wielders of the lash,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Wherewith the foremost Bolgia was replete.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Down at the bottom were the sinners naked;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* This side the middle came they facing us,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Beyond it, with us, but with greater steps;
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Even as the Romans, for the mighty host,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* The year of Jubilee, upon the bridge,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Have chosen a mode to pass the people over;
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* For all upon one side towards the Castle
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Their faces have, and go unto St. Peter’s;
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* On the other side they go towards the Mountain.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* This side and that, along the livid stone
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Beheld I horned demons with great scourges,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Who cruelly were beating them behind.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Ah me! how they did make them lift their legs
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* At the first blows! and sooth not any one
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* The second waited for, nor for the third.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* While I was going on, mine eyes by one
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Encountered were; and straight I said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Already\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*With sight of this one I am not unfed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Therefore I stayed my feet to make him out,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* And with me the sweet Guide came to a stand,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And to my going somewhat back assented;
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And he, the scourged one, thought to hide himself,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Lowering his face, but little it availed him;
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* For said I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Thou that castest down thine eyes,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If false are not the features which thou bearest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou art Venedico Caccianimico;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But what doth bring thee to such pungent sauces?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*“Unwillingly I tell it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*But forces me thine utterance distinct,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Which makes me recollect the ancient world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*I was the one who the fair Ghisola\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Induced to grant the wishes of the Marquis,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Howe’er the shameless story may be told.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Not the sole Bolognese am I who weeps here;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Nay, rather is this place so full of them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*That not so many tongues to-day are taught\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*’Twixt Reno and Savena to say ‘sipa;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*And if thereof thou wishest pledge or proof,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Venedico Caccianemico"\*Bring to thy mind our avaricious heart.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* While speaking in this manner, with his scourge
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* A demon smote him, and said: \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*“Get thee gone\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*Pander, there are no women here for coin.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* I joined myself again unto mine Escort;
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Thereafterward with footsteps few we came
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* To where a crag projected from the bank.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* This very easily did we ascend,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And turning to the right along its ridge,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* From those eternal circles we departed.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* When we were there, where it is hollowed out
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Beneath, to give a passage to the scourged,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* The Guide said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Wait, and see that on thee strike\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The vision of those others evil-born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of whom thou hast not yet beheld the faces,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because together with us they have gone.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* From the old bridge we looked upon the train
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Which tow’rds us came upon the other border,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* And which the scourges in like manner smite.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And the good Master, without my inquiring,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“See that tall one who is coming,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And for his pain seems not to shed a tear;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still what a royal aspect he retains!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That Jason is, who by his heart and cunning\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The Colchians of the Ram made destitute.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He by the isle of Lemnos passed along\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After the daring women pitiless\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Had unto death devoted all their males.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There with his tokens and with ornate words\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Did he deceive Hypsipyle, the maiden\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who first, herself, had all the rest deceived.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There did he leave her pregnant and forlorn;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Such sin unto such punishment condemns him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And also for Medea is vengeance done.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With him go those who in such wise deceive;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And this sufficient be of the first valley\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To know, and those that in its jaws it holds.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* We were already where the narrow path
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Crosses athwart the second dike, and forms
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Of that a buttress for another arch.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Thence we heard people, who are making moan
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* In the next Bolgia, snorting with their muzzles,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* And with their palms beating upon themselves
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* The margins were incrusted with a mould
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* By exhalation from below, that sticks there,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* And with the eyes and nostrils wages war.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* The bottom is so deep, no place suffices
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* To give us sight of it, without ascending
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* The arch’s back, where most the crag impends.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Thither we came, and thence down in the moat
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* I saw a people smothered in a filth
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* That out of human privies seemed to flow;
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* And whilst below there with mine eye I search,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* I saw one with his head so foul with ordure,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* It was not clear if he were clerk or layman.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* He screamed to me: \zsay-s |who="Alessio Interminei"\*“Wherefore art thou so eager\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alessio Interminei"\*To look at me more than the other foul ones?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Because, if I remember,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I have already seen thee with dry hair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And thou’rt Alessio Interminei of Lucca;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore I eye thee more than all the others.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And he thereon, belabouring his pumpkin:
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alessio Interminei"\*“The flatteries have submerged me here below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alessio Interminei"\*Wherewith my tongue was never surfeited.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Then said to me the Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“See that thou thrust\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy visage somewhat farther in advance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That with thine eyes thou well the face attain\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of that uncleanly and dishevelled drab,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who there doth scratch herself with filthy nails,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And crouches now, and now on foot is standing.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thais the harlot is it, who replied\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Unto her paramour, when he said, ‘Have I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Great gratitude from thee?’—‘Nay, marvellous;’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And herewith let our sight be satisfied.”\zsay-e\*
\c 19
\cp 19
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* O Simon Magus, O forlorn disciples,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Ye who the things of God, which ought to be
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* The brides of holiness, rapaciously
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* For silver and for gold do prostitute,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Now it behoves for you the trumpet sound,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Because in this third Bolgia ye abide.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* We had already on the following tomb
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Ascended to that portion of the crag
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Which o’er the middle of the moat hangs plumb.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Wisdom supreme, O how great art thou showest
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* In heaven, in earth, and in the evil world,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And with what justice doth thy power distribute!
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* I saw upon the sides and on the bottom
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The livid stone with perforations filled,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* All of one size, and every one was round.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* To me less ample seemed they not, nor greater
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Than those that in my beautiful Saint John
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Are fashioned for the place of the baptisers,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And one of which, not many years ago,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* I broke for some one, who was drowning in it;
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Be this a seal all men to undeceive.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Out of the mouth of each one there protruded
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* The feet of a transgressor, and the legs
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Up to the calf, the rest within remained.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* In all of them the soles were both on fire;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Wherefore the joints so violently quivered,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* They would have snapped asunder withes and bands.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Even as the flame of unctuous things is wont
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* To move upon the outer surface only,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* So likewise was it there from heel to point.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, who is that one who writhes himself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*More than his other comrades quivering,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“and whom a redder flame is sucking?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If thou wilt have me bear thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Down there along that bank which lowest lies,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From him thou’lt know his errors and himself.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“What pleases thee, to me is pleasing;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou art my Lord, and knowest that I depart not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From thy desire, and knowest what is not spoken.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Straightway upon the fourth dike we arrived;
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* We turned, and on the left-hand side descended
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Down to the bottom full of holes and narrow.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And the good Master yet from off his haunch
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Deposed me not, till to the hole he brought me
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Of him who so lamented with his shanks.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Whoe’er thou art, that standest upside down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*O doleful soul, implanted like a stake,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* To say began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“if thou canst, speak out.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* I stood even as the friar who is confessing
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* The false assassin, who, when he is fixed,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Recalls him, so that death may be delayed.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And he cried out: \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*“Dost thou stand there already,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Dost thou stand there already, Boniface?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*By many years the record lied to me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Art thou so early satiate with that wealth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*For which thou didst not fear to take by fraud\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*The beautiful Lady, and then work her woe?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Such I became, as people are who stand,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Not comprehending what is answered them,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* As if bemocked, and know not how to answer.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Then said Virgilius: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Say to him straightway,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*‘I am not he, I am not he thou thinkest.’”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* And I replied as was imposed on me.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Whereat the spirit writhed with both his feet,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Then, sighing, with a voice of lamentation
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Said to me: \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*“Then what wantest thou of me?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*If who I am thou carest so much to know,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*That thou on that account hast crossed the bank,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Know that I vested was with the great mantle;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*And truly was I son of the She-bear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*So eager to advance the cubs, that wealth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Above, and here myself, I pocketed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Beneath my head the others are dragged down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Who have preceded me in simony,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Flattened along the fissure of the rock.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Below there I shall likewise fall, whenever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*That one shall come who I believed thou wast,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*What time the sudden question I proposed.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*But longer I my feet already toast,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*And here have been in this way upside down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Than he will planted stay with reddened feet;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*For after him shall come of fouler deed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*From tow’rds the west a Pastor without law,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*Such as befits to cover him and me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*New Jason will he be, of whom we read\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*In Maccabees; and as his king was pliant,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nicholas III"\*So he who governs France shall be to this one.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* I do not know if I were here too bold,
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* That him I answered only in this metre:
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I pray thee tell me now how great a treasure\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Our Lord demanded of Saint Peter first,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Before he put the keys into his keeping?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Truly he nothing asked but ‘Follow me.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Nor Peter nor the rest asked of Matthias\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Silver or gold, when he by lot was chosen\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Unto the place the guilty soul had lost.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Therefore stay here, for thou art justly punished,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And keep safe guard o’er the ill-gotten money,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which caused thee to be valiant against Charles.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And were it not that still forbids it me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The reverence for the keys superlative\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou hadst in keeping in the gladsome life,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I would make use of words more grievous still;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Because your avarice afflicts the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Trampling the good and lifting the depraved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The Evangelist you Pastors had in mind,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*When she who sitteth upon many waters\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To fornicate with kings by him was seen;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The same who with the seven heads was born,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And power and strength from the ten horns received,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So long as virtue to her spouse was pleasing.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ye have made yourselves a god of gold and silver;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And from the idolater how differ ye,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Save that he one, and ye a hundred worship?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ah, Constantine! of how much ill was mother,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Not thy conversion, but that marriage dower\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which the first wealthy Father took from thee!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* And while I sang to him such notes as these,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Either that anger or that conscience stung him,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* He struggled violently with both his feet.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* I think in sooth that it my Leader pleased,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* With such contented lip he listened ever
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Unto the sound of the true words expressed.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Therefore with both his arms he took me up,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* And when he had me all upon his breast,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Remounted by the way where he descended.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Nor did he tire to have me clasped to him;
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* But bore me to the summit of the arch
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Which from the fourth dike to the fifth is passage.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* There tenderly he laid his burden down,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Tenderly on the crag uneven and steep,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* That would have been hard passage for the goats:
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Thence was unveiled to me another valley.
\c 20
\cp 20
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Of a new pain behoves me to make verses
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* And give material to the twentieth canto
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Of the first song, which is of the submerged.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* I was already thoroughly disposed
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* To peer down into the uncovered depth,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Which bathed itself with tears of agony;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And people saw I through the circular valley,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Silent and weeping, coming at the pace
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Which in this world the Litanies assume.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* As lower down my sight descended on them,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Wondrously each one seemed to be distorted
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* From chin to the beginning of the chest;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* For tow’rds the reins the countenance was turned,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* And backward it behoved them to advance,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* As to look forward had been taken from them.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Perchance indeed by violence of palsy
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Some one has been thus wholly turned awry;
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* But I ne’er saw it, nor believe it can be.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* As God may let thee, Reader, gather fruit
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* From this thy reading, think now for thyself
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* How I could ever keep my face unmoistened,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* When our own image near me I beheld
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Distorted so, the weeping of the eyes
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Along the fissure bathed the hinder parts.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Truly I wept, leaning upon a peak
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Of the hard crag, so that my Escort said
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* To me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Art thou, too, of the other fools?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here pity lives when it is wholly dead;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who is a greater reprobate than he\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who feels compassion at the doom divine?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Lift up, lift up thy head, and see for whom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Opened the earth before the Thebans’ eyes;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Wherefore they all cried: ‘Whither rushest thou,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Amphiaraus? Why dost leave the war?’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And downward ceased he not to fall amain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As far as Minos, who lays hold on all.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See, he has made a bosom of his shoulders!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because he wished to see too far before him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behind he looks, and backward goes his way:\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold Tiresias, who his semblance changed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When from a male a female he became,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*His members being all of them transformed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And afterwards was forced to strike once more\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The two entangled serpents with his rod,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ere he could have again his manly plumes.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That Aruns is, who backs the other’s belly,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who in the hills of Luni, there where grubs\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The Carrarese who houses underneath,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among the marbles white a cavern had\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For his abode; whence to behold the stars\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And sea, the view was not cut off from him.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And she there, who is covering up her breasts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which thou beholdest not, with loosened tresses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And on that side has all the hairy skin,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was Manto, who made quest through many lands,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Afterwards tarried there where I was born;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whereof I would thou list to me a little.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*After her father had from life departed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And the city of Bacchus had become enslaved,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She a long season wandered through the world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Above in beauteous Italy lies a lake\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*At the Alp’s foot that shuts in Germany\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Over Tyrol, and has the name Benaco.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By a thousand springs, I think, and more, is bathed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Twixt Garda and Val Camonica, Pennino,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With water that grows stagnant in that lake.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Midway a place is where the Trentine Pastor,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And he of Brescia, and the Veronese\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Might give his blessing, if he passed that way.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Sitteth Peschiera, fortress fair and strong,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To front the Brescians and the Bergamasks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where round about the bank descendeth lowest.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There of necessity must fall whatever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In bosom of Benaco cannot stay,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And grows a river down through verdant pastures.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Soon as the water doth begin to run,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No more Benaco is it called, but Mincio,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Far as Governo, where it falls in Po.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Not far it runs before it finds a plain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In which it spreads itself, and makes it marshy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And oft ’tis wont in summer to be sickly.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Passing that way the virgin pitiless\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Land in the middle of the fen descried,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Untilled and naked of inhabitants;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There to escape all human intercourse,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*She with her servants stayed, her arts to practise\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And lived, and left her empty body there.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The men, thereafter, who were scattered round,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Collected in that place, which was made strong\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By the lagoon it had on every side;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They built their city over those dead bones,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And, after her who first the place selected,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Mantua named it, without other omen.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Its people once within more crowded were,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ere the stupidity of Casalodi\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From Pinamonte had received deceit.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore I caution thee, if e’er thou hearest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Originate my city otherwise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*No falsehood may the verity defraud.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, thy discourses are\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To me so certain, and so take my faith,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That unto me the rest would be spent coals.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But tell me of the people who are passing,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If any one note-worthy thou beholdest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For only unto that my mind reverts.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Then said he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“He who from the cheek\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thrusts out his beard upon his swarthy shoulders\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was, at the time when Greece was void of males,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that there scarce remained one in the cradle,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*An augur, and with Calchas gave the moment,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*In Aulis, when to sever the first cable.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Eurypylus his name was, and so sings\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*My lofty Tragedy in some part or other;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That knowest thou well, who knowest the whole of it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The next, who is so slender in the flanks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Was Michael Scott, who of a verity\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of magical illusions knew the game.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold Guido Bonatti, behold Asdente,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who now unto his leather and his thread\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Would fain have stuck, but he too late repents.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behold the wretched ones, who left the needle,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The spool and rock, and made them fortune-tellers;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They wrought their magic spells with herb and image.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But come now, for already holds the confines\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of both the hemispheres, and under Seville\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Touches the ocean-wave, Cain and the thorns,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And yesternight the moon was round already;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou shouldst remember well it did not harm thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From time to time within the forest deep.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Thus spake he to me, and we walked the while.
\c 21
\cp 21
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* From bridge to bridge thus, speaking other things
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Of which my Comedy cares not to sing,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* We came along, and held the summit, when
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* We halted to behold another fissure
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Of Malebolge and other vain laments;
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And I beheld it marvellously dark.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* As in the Arsenal of the Venetians
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Boils in the winter the tenacious pitch
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* To smear their unsound vessels o’er again,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* For sail they cannot; and instead thereof
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* One makes his vessel new, and one recaulks
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* The ribs of that which many a voyage has made;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* One hammers at the prow, one at the stern,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* This one makes oars, and that one cordage twists,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Another mends the mainsail and the mizzen;
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Thus, not by fire, but by the art divine,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Was boiling down below there a dense pitch
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Which upon every side the bank belimed.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I saw it, but I did not see within it
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Aught but the bubbles that the boiling raised,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And all swell up and resubside compressed.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* The while below there fixedly I gazed,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* My Leader, crying out: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Beware, beware!”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Drew me unto himself from where I stood.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Then I turned round, as one who is impatient
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* To see what it behoves him to escape,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* And whom a sudden terror doth unman,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Who, while he looks, delays not his departure;
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And I beheld behind us a black devil,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Running along upon the crag, approach.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Ah, how ferocious was he in his aspect!
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* And how he seemed to me in action ruthless,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* With open wings and light upon his feet!
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* His shoulders, which sharp-pointed were and high,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* A sinner did encumber with both haunches,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And he held clutched the sinews of the feet.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* From off our bridge, he said: \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*“O Malebranche,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*Behold one of the elders of Saint Zita;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*Plunge him beneath, for I return for others\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*Unto that town, which is well furnished with them.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*All there are barrators, except Bonturo;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="A demon"\*No into Yes for money there is changed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* He hurled him down, and over the hard crag
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Turned round, and never was a mastiff loosened
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* In so much hurry to pursue a thief.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* The other sank, and rose again face downward;
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* But the demons, under cover of the bridge,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Cried: \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“Here the Santo Volto has no place!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*Here swims one otherwise than in the Serchio;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*Therefore, if for our gaffs thou wishest not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*Do not uplift thyself above the pitch.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* They seized him then with more than a hundred rakes;
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* They said: \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“It here behoves thee to dance covered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*That, if thou canst, thou secretly mayest pilfer.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Not otherwise the cooks their scullions make
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Immerse into the middle of the caldron
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* The meat with hooks, so that it may not float.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Said the good Master to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“That it be not\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Apparent thou art here, crouch thyself down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Behind a jag, that thou mayest have some screen;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And for no outrage that is done to me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Be thou afraid, because these things I know,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For once before was I in such a scuffle.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Then he passed on beyond the bridge’s head,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* And as upon the sixth bank he arrived,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Need was for him to have a steadfast front.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* With the same fury, and the same uproar,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* As dogs leap out upon a mendicant,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Who on a sudden begs, where’er he stops,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* They issued from beneath the little bridge,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And turned against him all their grappling-irons;
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* But he cried out: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be none of you malignant!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Before those hooks of yours lay hold of me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let one of you step forward, who may hear me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And then take counsel as to grappling me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* They all cried out: \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“Let Malacoda go;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Whereat one started, and the rest stood still,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* And he came to him, saying: \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*“What avails it?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thinkest thou, Malacoda, to behold me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Advanced into this place,”\zsay-e\* my Master said,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Safe hitherto from all your skill of fence,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Without the will divine, and fate auspicious?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let me go on, for it in Heaven is willed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That I another show this savage road.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Then was his arrogance so humbled in him,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* That he let fall his grapnel at his feet,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* And to the others said: \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*“Now strike him not.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* And unto me my Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O thou, who sittest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among the splinters of the bridge crouched down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Securely now return to me again.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Wherefore I started and came swiftly to him;
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* And all the devils forward thrust themselves,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* So that I feared they would not keep their compact.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And thus beheld I once afraid the soldiers
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Who issued under safeguard from Caprona,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Seeing themselves among so many foes.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Close did I press myself with all my person
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* Beside my Leader, and turned not mine eyes
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* From off their countenance, which was not good.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* They lowered their rakes, and \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“Wilt thou have me hit him,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* They said to one another, \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“on the rump?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* And answered: \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“Yes; see that thou nick him with it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* But the same demon who was holding parley
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* With my Conductor turned him very quickly,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*“Be quiet, be quiet, Scarmiglione;”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Then said to us: \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*“You can no farther go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Forward upon this crag, because is lying\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*All shattered, at the bottom, the sixth arch.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*And if it still doth please you to go onward,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Pursue your way along upon this rock;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Near is another crag that yields a path.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Yesterday, five hours later than this hour,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*One thousand and two hundred sixty-six\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Years were complete, that here the way was broken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*I send in that direction some of mine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*To see if any one doth air himself;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Go ye with them; for they will not be vicious.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Step forward, Alichino and Calcabrina,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Began he to cry out, \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*“and thou, Cagnazzo;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*And Barbariccia, do thou guide the ten.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Come forward, Libicocco and Draghignazzo,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*And tusked Ciriatto and Graffiacane,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*And Farfarello and mad Rubicante;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Search ye all round about the boiling pitch;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*Let these be safe as far as the next crag,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Malacoda"\*That all unbroken passes o’er the dens.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O me! what is it, Master, that I see?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Pray let us go,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“without an escort,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If thou knowest how, since for myself I ask none.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If thou art as observant as thy wont is,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Dost thou not see that they do gnash their teeth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And with their brows are threatening woe to us?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I will not have thee fear;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let them gnash on, according to their fancy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because they do it for those boiling wretches.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* Along the left-hand dike they wheeled about;
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* But first had each one thrust his tongue between
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* His teeth towards their leader for a signal;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* And he had made a trumpet of his rump.
\c 22
\cp 22
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* I have erewhile seen horsemen moving camp,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Begin the storming, and their muster make,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And sometimes starting off for their escape;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Vaunt-couriers have I seen upon your land,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* O Aretines, and foragers go forth,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Tournaments stricken, and the joustings run,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Sometimes with trumpets and sometimes with bells,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* With kettle-drums, and signals of the castles,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And with our own, and with outlandish things,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* But never yet with bagpipe so uncouth
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Did I see horsemen move, nor infantry,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Nor ship by any sign of land or star.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* We went upon our way with the ten demons;
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Ah, savage company! but in the church
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* With saints, and in the tavern with the gluttons!
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Ever upon the pitch was my intent,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* To see the whole condition of that Bolgia,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And of the people who therein were burned.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Even as the dolphins, when they make a sign
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* To mariners by arching of the back,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* That they should counsel take to save their vessel,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Thus sometimes, to alleviate his pain,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* One of the sinners would display his back,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* And in less time conceal it than it lightens.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As on the brink of water in a ditch
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* The frogs stand only with their muzzles out,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* So that they hide their feet and other bulk,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* So upon every side the sinners stood;
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* But ever as Barbariccia near them came,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Thus underneath the boiling they withdrew.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* I saw, and still my heart doth shudder at it,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* One waiting thus, even as it comes to pass
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* One frog remains, and down another dives;
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And Graffiacan, who most confronted him,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Grappled him by his tresses smeared with pitch,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* And drew him up, so that he seemed an otter.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* I knew, before, the names of all of them,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* So had I noted them when they were chosen,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* And when they called each other, listened how.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*“O Rubicante, see that thou do lay\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="The Malebranche"\*Thy claws upon him, so that thou mayst flay him,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Cried all together the accursed ones.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, see to it, if thou canst,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That thou mayst know who is the luckless wight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thus come into his adversaries’ hands.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Near to the side of him my Leader drew,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Asked of him whence he was; and he replied:
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“I in the kingdom of Navarre was born;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*My mother placed me servant to a lord,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*For she had borne me to a ribald knave,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Destroyer of himself and of his things.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Then I domestic was of good King Thibault;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*I set me there to practise barratry,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*For which I pay the reckoning in this heat.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* And Ciriatto, from whose mouth projected,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* On either side, a tusk, as in a boar,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Caused him to feel how one of them could rip.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Among malicious cats the mouse had come;
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* But Barbariccia clasped him in his arms,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Barbariccia"\*“Stand ye aside, while I enfork him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* And to my Master he turned round his head;
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Barbariccia"\*“Ask him again,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Barbariccia"\*“if more thou wish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Barbariccia"\*To know from him, before some one destroy him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* The Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now tell then of the other culprits;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Knowest thou any one who is a Latian,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Under the pitch?”\zsay-e\* And he: \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“I separated\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Lately from one who was a neighbour to it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Would that I still were covered up with him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*For I should fear not either claw nor hook!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And Libicocco: \zsay-s |who="Libicocco"\*“We have borne too much;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And with his grapnel seized him by the arm,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* So that, by rending, he tore off a tendon.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Eke Draghignazzo wished to pounce upon him
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Down at the legs; whence their Decurion
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Turned round and round about with evil look.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When they again somewhat were pacified,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Of him, who still was looking at his wound,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Demanded my Conductor without stay:
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Who was that one, from whom a luckless parting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou sayest thou hast made, to come ashore?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* And he replied: \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“It was the Friar Gomita,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*He of Gallura, vessel of all fraud,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Who had the enemies of his Lord in hand,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*And dealt so with them each exults thereat;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Money he took, and let them smoothly off,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*As he says; and in other offices\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*A barrator was he, not mean but sovereign.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Foregathers with him one Don Michael Zanche\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Of Logodoro; and of Sardinia\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*To gossip never do their tongues feel tired.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*O me! see that one, how he grinds his teeth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Still farther would I speak, but am afraid\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*Lest he to scratch my itch be making ready.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And the grand Provost, turned to Farfarello,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Who rolled his eyes about as if to strike,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Barbariccia"\*“Stand aside there, thou malicious bird.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“If you desire either to see or hear,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* The terror-stricken recommenced thereon,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“Tuscans or Lombards, I will make them come.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*But let the Malebranche cease a little,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*So that these may not their revenges fear,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*And I, down sitting in this very place,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*For one that I am will make seven come,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*When I shall whistle, as our custom is\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*To do whenever one of us comes out.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Cagnazzo at these words his muzzle lifted,
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Shaking his head, and said: \zsay-s |who="Cagnazzo"\*“Just hear the trick\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Cagnazzo"\*Which he has thought of, down to throw himself!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Whence he, who snares in great abundance had,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Responded: \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*“I by far too cunning am,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ciampolo"\*When I procure for mine a greater sadness.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Alichin held not in, but running counter
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Unto the rest, said to him: \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*“If thou dive,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*I will not follow thee upon the gallop,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*But I will beat my wings above the pitch;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*The height be left, and be the bank a shield\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*To see if thou alone dost countervail us.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* O thou who readest, thou shalt hear new sport!
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Each to the other side his eyes averted;
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* He first, who most reluctant was to do it.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* The Navarrese selected well his time;
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Planted his feet on land, and in a moment
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Leaped, and released himself from their design.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Whereat each one was suddenly stung with shame,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* But he most who was cause of the defeat;
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Therefore he moved, and cried: \zsay-s |who="Alichino"\*“Thou art o’ertaken.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* But little it availed, for wings could not
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Outstrip the fear; the other one went under,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* And, flying, upward he his breast directed;
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Not otherwise the duck upon a sudden
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Dives under, when the falcon is approaching,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* And upward he returneth cross and weary.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Infuriate at the mockery, Calcabrina
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Flying behind him followed close, desirous
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* The other should escape, to have a quarrel.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And when the barrator had disappeared,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* He turned his talons upon his companion,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And grappled with him right above the moat.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* But sooth the other was a doughty sparhawk
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* To clapperclaw him well; and both of them
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Fell in the middle of the boiling pond.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* A sudden intercessor was the heat;
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* But ne’ertheless of rising there was naught,
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* To such degree they had their wings belimed.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Lamenting with the others, Barbariccia
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Made four of them fly to the other side
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* With all their gaffs, and very speedily
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* This side and that they to their posts descended;
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* They stretched their hooks towards the pitch-ensnared,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* Who were already baked within the crust,
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* And in this manner busied did we leave them.
\c 23
\cp 23
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Silent, alone, and without company
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* We went, the one in front, the other after,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As go the Minor Friars along their way.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Upon the fable of Aesop was directed
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* My thought, by reason of the present quarrel,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Where he has spoken of the frog and mouse;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* For ‘mo’ and ‘issa’ are not more alike
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Than this one is to that, if well we couple
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* End and beginning with a steadfast mind.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And even as one thought from another springs,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* So afterward from that was born another,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Which the first fear within me double made.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Thus did I ponder: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“These on our account\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Are laughed to scorn, with injury and scoff\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So great, that much I think it must annoy them.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If anger be engrafted on ill-will,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*They will come after us more merciless\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Than dog upon the leveret which he seizes,”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I felt my hair stand all on end already
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* With terror, and stood backwardly intent,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* When said I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, if thou hidest not\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thyself and me forthwith, of Malebranche\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I am in dread; we have them now behind us;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I so imagine them, I already feel them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And he: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“If I were made of leaded glass,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thine outward image I should not attract\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Sooner to me than I imprint the inner.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Just now thy thoughts came in among my own,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With similar attitude and similar face,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So that of both one counsel sole I made.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If peradventure the right bank so slope\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That we to the next Bolgia can descend,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*We shall escape from the imagined chase.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Not yet he finished rendering such opinion,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* When I beheld them come with outstretched wings,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Not far remote, with will to seize upon us.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* My Leader on a sudden seized me up,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Even as a mother who by noise is wakened,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* And close beside her sees the enkindled flames,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Who takes her son, and flies, and does not stop,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Having more care of him than of herself,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* So that she clothes her only with a shift;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And downward from the top of the hard bank
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Supine he gave him to the pendent rock,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* That one side of the other Bolgia walls.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Ne’er ran so swiftly water through a sluice
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* To turn the wheel of any land-built mill,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* When nearest to the paddles it approaches,
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* As did my Master down along that border,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Bearing me with him on his breast away,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* As his own son, and not as a companion.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Hardly the bed of the ravine below
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* His feet had reached, ere they had reached the hill
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Right over us; but he was not afraid;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* For the high Providence, which had ordained
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* To place them ministers of the fifth moat,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* The power of thence departing took from all.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* A painted people there below we found,
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Who went about with footsteps very slow,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Weeping and in their semblance tired and vanquished.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* They had on mantles with the hoods low down
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Before their eyes, and fashioned of the cut
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* That in Cologne they for the monks are made.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Without, they gilded are so that it dazzles;
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* But inwardly all leaden and so heavy
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* That Frederick used to put them on of straw.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* O everlastingly fatiguing mantle!
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Again we turned us, still to the left hand
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Along with them, intent on their sad plaint;
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* But owing to the weight, that weary folk
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Came on so tardily, that we were new
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* In company at each motion of the haunch.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Whence I unto my Leader: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“See thou find\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Some one who may by deed or name be known,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And thus in going move thine eye about.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And one, who understood the Tuscan speech,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Cried to us from behind: \zsay-s |who="A hypocrite"\*“Stay ye your feet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="A hypocrite"\*Ye, who so run athwart the dusky air!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="A hypocrite"\*Perhaps thou’lt have from me what thou demandest.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Whereat the Leader turned him, and said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Wait,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And then according to his pace proceed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* I stopped, and two beheld I show great haste
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Of spirit, in their faces, to be with me;
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* But the burden and the narrow way delayed them.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* When they came up, long with an eye askance
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* They scanned me without uttering a word.
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Then to each other turned, and said together:
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*“He by the action of his throat seems living;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*And if they dead are, by what privilege\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*Go they uncovered by the heavy stole?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Then said to me: \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*“Tuscan, who to the college\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*Of miserable hypocrites art come,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="The two friars"\*Do not disdain to tell us who thou art.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And I to them: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Born was I, and grew up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In the great town on the fair river of Arno,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And with the body am I’ve always had.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But who are ye, in whom there trickles down\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Along your cheeks such grief as I behold?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And what pain is upon you, that so sparkles?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And one replied to me: \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*“These orange cloaks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Are made of lead so heavy, that the weights\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Cause in this way their balances to creak.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Frati Gaudenti were we, and Bolognese;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*I Catalano, and he Loderingo\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Named, and together taken by thy city,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*As the wont is to take one man alone,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*For maintenance of its peace; and we were such\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*That still it is apparent round Gardingo.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O Friars,”\zsay-e\* began I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“your iniquitous. . .”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* But said no more; for to mine eyes there rushed
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* One crucified with three stakes on the ground.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* When me he saw, he writhed himself all over,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* Blowing into his beard with suspirations;
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* And the Friar Catalan, who noticed this,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Said to me: \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*“This transfixed one, whom thou seest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Counselled the Pharisees that it was meet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*To put one man to torture for the people.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Crosswise and naked is he on the path,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*As thou perceivest; and he needs must feel,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Whoever passes, first how much he weighs;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*And in like mode his father-in-law is punished\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Within this moat, and the others of the council,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Which for the Jews was a malignant seed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* And thereupon I saw Virgilius marvel
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* O’er him who was extended on the cross
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* So vilely in eternal banishment.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* Then he directed to the Friar this voice:
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be not displeased, if granted thee, to tell us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If to the right hand any pass slope down\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By which we two may issue forth from here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Without constraining some of the black angels\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To come and extricate us from this deep.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Then he made answer: \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*“Nearer than thou hopest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*There is a rock, that forth from the great circle\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Proceeds, and crosses all the cruel valleys,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Save that at this ’tis broken, and does not bridge it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*You will be able to mount up the ruin,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*That sidelong slopes and at the bottom rises.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* The Leader stood awhile with head bowed down;
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Then said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“The business badly he recounted\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who grapples with his hook the sinners yonder.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* And the Friar: \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*“Many of the Devil’s vices\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*Once heard I at Bologna, and among them,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Catalano"\*That he’s a liar and the father of lies.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* Thereat my Leader with great strides went on,
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Somewhat disturbed with anger in his looks;
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* Whence from the heavy-laden I departed
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* After the prints of his beloved feet.
\c 24
\cp 24
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* In that part of the youthful year wherein
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* The Sun his locks beneath Aquarius tempers,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And now the nights draw near to half the day,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* What time the hoar-frost copies on the ground
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* The outward semblance of her sister white,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* But little lasts the temper of her pen,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* The husbandman, whose forage faileth him,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Rises, and looks, and seeth the champaign
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* All gleaming white, whereat he beats his flank,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Returns in doors, and up and down laments,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Like a poor wretch, who knows not what to do;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Then he returns and hope revives again,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Seeing the world has changed its countenance
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* In little time, and takes his shepherd’s crook,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* And forth the little lambs to pasture drives.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Thus did the Master fill me with alarm,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* When I beheld his forehead so disturbed,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And to the ailment came as soon the plaster.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* For as we came unto the ruined bridge,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* The Leader turned to me with that sweet look
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Which at the mountain’s foot I first beheld.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* His arms he opened, after some advisement
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Within himself elected, looking first
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Well at the ruin, and laid hold of me.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* And even as he who acts and meditates,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* For aye it seems that he provides beforehand,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* So upward lifting me towards the summit
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Of a huge rock, he scanned another crag,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“To that one grapple afterwards,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But try first if ’tis such that it will hold thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* This was no way for one clothed with a cloak;
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* For hardly we, he light, and I pushed upward,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Were able to ascend from jag to jag.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And had it not been, that upon that precinct
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Shorter was the ascent than on the other,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* He I know not, but I had been dead beat.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* But because Malebolge tow’rds the mouth
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Of the profoundest well is all inclining,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* The structure of each valley doth import
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* That one bank rises and the other sinks.
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Still we arrived at length upon the point
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Wherefrom the last stone breaks itself asunder.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* The breath was from my lungs so milked away,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* When I was up, that I could go no farther,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Nay, I sat down upon my first arrival.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now it behoves thee thus to put off sloth,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* My Master said; \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“for sitting upon down,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Or under quilt, one cometh not to fame,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Withouten which whoso his life consumes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Such vestige leaveth of himself on earth,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As smoke in air or in the water foam.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And therefore raise thee up, o’ercome the anguish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With spirit that o’ercometh every battle,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If with its heavy body it sink not.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*A longer stairway it behoves thee mount;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*’Tis not enough from these to have departed;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Let it avail thee, if thou understand me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Then I uprose, showing myself provided
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Better with breath than I did feel myself,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Go on, for I am strong and bold.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Upward we took our way along the crag,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Which jagged was, and narrow, and difficult,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* And more precipitous far than that before.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Speaking I went, not to appear exhausted;
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Whereat a voice from the next moat came forth,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Not well adapted to articulate words.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* I know not what it said, though o’er the back
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* I now was of the arch that passes there;
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* But he seemed moved to anger who was speaking.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* I was bent downward, but my living eyes
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Could not attain the bottom, for the dark;
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Wherefore I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, see that thou arrive\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*At the next round, and let us descend the wall;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For as from hence I hear and understand not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So I look down and nothing I distinguish.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Other response,”\zsay-e\* he said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“I make thee not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Except the doing; for the modest asking\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ought to be followed by the deed in silence.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* We from the bridge descended at its head,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Where it connects itself with the eighth bank,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* And then was manifest to me the Bolgia;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And I beheld therein a terrible throng
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Of serpents, and of such a monstrous kind,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* That the remembrance still congeals my blood
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Let Libya boast no longer with her sand;
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* For if Chelydri, Jaculi, and Phareae
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* She breeds, with Cenchri and with Amphisbaena,
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Neither so many plagues nor so malignant
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* E’er showed she with all Ethiopia,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Nor with whatever on the Red Sea is!
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* Among this cruel and most dismal throng
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* People were running naked and affrighted.
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Without the hope of hole or heliotrope.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* They had their hands with serpents bound behind them;
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* These riveted upon their reins the tail
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And head, and were in front of them entwined.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And lo! at one who was upon our side
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* There darted forth a serpent, which transfixed him
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* There where the neck is knotted to the shoulders.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Nor ‘O’ so quickly e’er, nor ‘I’ was written,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* As he took fire, and burned; and ashes wholly
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Behoved it that in falling he became.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And when he on the ground was thus destroyed,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The ashes drew together, and of themselves
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* Into himself they instantly returned.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Even thus by the great sages ’tis confessed
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* The phoenix dies, and then is born again,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* When it approaches its five-hundredth year;
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* On herb or grain it feeds not in its life,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* But only on tears of incense and amomum,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* And nard and myrrh are its last winding-sheet.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* And as he is who falls, and knows not how,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* By force of demons who to earth down drag him,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Or other oppilation that binds man,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* When he arises and around him looks,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Wholly bewildered by the mighty anguish
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Which he has suffered, and in looking sighs;
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Such was that sinner after he had risen.
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* Justice of God! O how severe it is,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* That blows like these in vengeance poureth down!
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* The Guide thereafter asked him who he was;
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Whence he replied: \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*“I rained from Tuscany\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*A short time since into this cruel gorge.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*A bestial life, and not a human, pleased me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Even as the mule I was; I’m Vanni Fucci,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Beast, and Pistoia was my worthy den.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And I unto the Guide: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Tell him to stir not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And ask what crime has thrust him here below,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For once a man of blood and wrath I saw him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* And the sinner, who had heard, dissembled not,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* But unto me directed mind and face,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* And with a melancholy shame was painted.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Then said: \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*“It pains me more that thou hast caught me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Amid this misery where thou seest me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Than when I from the other life was taken.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*What thou demandest I cannot deny;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*So low am I put down because I robbed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*The sacristy of the fair ornaments,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*And falsely once ’twas laid upon another;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*But that thou mayst not such a sight enjoy,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*If thou shalt e’er be out of the dark places,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Thine ears to my announcement ope and hear:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Pistoia first of Neri groweth meagre;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Then Florence doth renew her men and manners;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Mars draws a vapour up from Val di Magra,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Which is with turbid clouds enveloped round,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*And with impetuous and bitter tempest\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*Over Campo Picen shall be the battle;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*When it shall suddenly rend the mist asunder,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*So that each Bianco shall thereby be smitten.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*And this I’ve said that it may give thee pain.”\zsay-e\*
\c 25
\cp 25
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* At the conclusion of his words, the thief
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Lifted his hands aloft with both the figs,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Crying: \zsay-s |who="Vanni Fucci"\*“Take that, God, for at thee I aim them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* From that time forth the serpents were my friends;
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* For one entwined itself about his neck
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* As if it said: \zsay-s |who="A serpent"\*“I will not thou speak more;”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* And round his arms another, and rebound him,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Clinching itself together so in front,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* That with them he could not a motion make.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Pistoia, ah, Pistoia! why resolve not
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* To burn thyself to ashes and so perish,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Since in ill-doing thou thy seed excellest?
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Through all the sombre circles of this Hell,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Spirit I saw not against God so proud,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Not he who fell at Thebes down from the walls!
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* He fled away, and spake no further word;
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* And I beheld a Centaur full of rage
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Come crying out: \zsay-s |who="Cacus"\*“Where is, where is the scoffer?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I do not think Maremma has so many
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Serpents as he had all along his back,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* As far as where our countenance begins.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Upon the shoulders, just behind the nape,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* With wings wide open was a dragon lying,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* And he sets fire to all that he encounters.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* My Master said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“That one is Cacus, who\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Beneath the rock upon Mount Aventine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Created oftentimes a lake of blood.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*He goes not on the same road with his brothers,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By reason of the fraudulent theft he made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the great herd, which he had near to him;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whereat his tortuous actions ceased beneath\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The mace of Hercules, who peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Gave him a hundred, and he felt not ten.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* While he was speaking thus, he had passed by,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* And spirits three had underneath us come,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Of which nor I aware was, nor my Leader,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Until what time they shouted: \zsay-s |who="The three thieves"\*“Who are you?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* On which account our story made a halt,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* And then we were intent on them alone.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* I did not know them; but it came to pass,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* As it is wont to happen by some chance,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* That one to name the other was compelled,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Exclaiming: \zsay-s |who="A thief"\*“Where can Cianfa have remained?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Whence I, so that the Leader might attend,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Upward from chin to nose my finger laid.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* If thou art, Reader, slow now to believe
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* What I shall say, it will no marvel be,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* For I who saw it hardly can admit it.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* As I was holding raised on them my brows,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Behold! a serpent with six feet darts forth
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* In front of one, and fastens wholly on him.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* With middle feet it bound him round the paunch,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* And with the forward ones his arms it seized;
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Then thrust its teeth through one cheek and the other;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* The hindermost it stretched upon his thighs,
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* And put its tail through in between the two,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* And up behind along the reins outspread it.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* Ivy was never fastened by its barbs
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Unto a tree so, as this horrible reptile
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Upon the other’s limbs entwined its own.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* Then they stuck close, as if of heated wax
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* They had been made, and intermixed their colour;
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Nor one nor other seemed now what he was;
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* E’en as proceedeth on before the flame
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Upward along the paper a brown colour,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Which is not black as yet, and the white dies.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* The other two looked on, and each of them
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Cried out: \zsay-s |who="Two thieves"\*“O me, Agnello, how thou changest!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Two thieves"\*Behold, thou now art neither two nor one.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Already the two heads had one become,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* When there appeared to us two figures mingled
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Into one face, wherein the two were lost.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* Of the four lists were fashioned the two arms,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* The thighs and legs, the belly and the chest
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Members became that never yet were seen.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Every original aspect there was cancelled;
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Two and yet none did the perverted image
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Appear, and such departed with slow pace.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Even as a lizard, under the great scourge
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of days canicular, exchanging hedge,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Lightning appeareth if the road it cross;
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Thus did appear, coming towards the bellies
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Of the two others, a small fiery serpent,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Livid and black as is a peppercorn.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* And in that part whereat is first received
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Our aliment, it one of them transfixed;
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Then downward fell in front of him extended.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* The one transfixed looked at it, but said naught;
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Nay, rather with feet motionless he yawned,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* Just as if sleep or fever had assailed him.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* He at the serpent gazed, and it at him;
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* One through the wound, the other through the mouth
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Smoked violently, and the smoke commingled.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Henceforth be silent Lucan, where he mentions
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Wretched Sabellus and Nassidius,
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* And wait to hear what now shall be shot forth.
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Be silent Ovid, of Cadmus and Arethusa;
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* For if him to a snake, her to a fountain,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Converts he fabling, that I grudge him not;
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Because two natures never front to front
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Has he transmuted, so that both the forms
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* To interchange their matter ready were.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Together they responded in such wise,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* That to a fork the serpent cleft his tail,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* And eke the wounded drew his feet together.
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* The legs together with the thighs themselves
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* Adhered so, that in little time the juncture
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* No sign whatever made that was apparent.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* He with the cloven tail assumed the figure
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* The other one was losing, and his skin
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Became elastic, and the other’s hard.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* I saw the arms draw inward at the armpits,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And both feet of the reptile, that were short,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Lengthen as much as those contracted were.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Thereafter the hind feet, together twisted,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Became the member that a man conceals,
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* And of his own the wretch had two created.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* While both of them the exhalation veils
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* With a new colour, and engenders hair
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* On one of them and depilates the other,
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* The one uprose and down the other fell,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Though turning not away their impious lamps,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* Underneath which each one his muzzle changed.
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* He who was standing drew it tow’rds the temples,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* And from excess of matter, which came thither,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* Issued the ears from out the hollow cheeks;
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* What did not backward run and was retained
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* Of that excess made to the face a nose,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* And the lips thickened far as was befitting.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* He who lay prostrate thrusts his muzzle forward,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* And backward draws the ears into his head,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* In the same manner as the snail its horns;
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* And so the tongue, which was entire and apt
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* For speech before, is cleft, and the bi-forked
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* In the other closes up, and the smoke ceases.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* The soul, which to a reptile had been changed,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Along the valley hissing takes to flight,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* And after him the other speaking sputters.
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Then did he turn upon him his new shoulders,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* And said to the other: \zsay-s |who="Francesco de' Cavalcanti"\*“I’ll have Buoso run,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Francesco de' Cavalcanti"\*Crawling as I have done, along this road.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* In this way I beheld the seventh ballast
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Shift and reshift, and here be my excuse
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* The novelty, if aught my pen transgress.
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* And notwithstanding that mine eyes might be
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* Somewhat bewildered, and my mind dismayed,
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* They could not flee away so secretly
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* But that I plainly saw Puccio Sciancato;
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* And he it was who sole of three companions,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* Which came in the beginning, was not changed;
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* The other was he whom thou, Gaville, weepest.
\c 26
\cp 26
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Rejoice, O Florence, since thou art so great,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* That over sea and land thou beatest thy wings,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And throughout Hell thy name is spread abroad!
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Among the thieves five citizens of thine
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Like these I found, whence shame comes unto me,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* And thou thereby to no great honour risest.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* But if when morn is near our dreams are true,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Feel shalt thou in a little time from now
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* What Prato, if none other, craves for thee.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* And if it now were, it were not too soon;
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Would that it were, seeing it needs must be,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* For ’twill aggrieve me more the more I age.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* We went our way, and up along the stairs
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The bourns had made us to descend before,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Remounted my Conductor and drew me.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* And following the solitary path
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Among the rocks and ridges of the crag,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The foot without the hand sped not at all.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Then sorrowed I, and sorrow now again,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* When I direct my mind to what I saw,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* And more my genius curb than I am wont,
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* That it may run not unless virtue guide it;
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* So that if some good star, or better thing,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Have given me good, I may myself not grudge it.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As many as the hind (who on the hill
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Rests at the time when he who lights the world
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* His countenance keeps least concealed from us,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* While as the fly gives place unto the gnat)
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Seeth the glow-worms down along the valley,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* Perchance there where he ploughs and makes his vintage;
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* With flames as manifold resplendent all
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Was the eighth Bolgia, as I grew aware
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* As soon as I was where the depth appeared.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And such as he who with the bears avenged him
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Beheld Elijah’s chariot at departing,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* What time the steeds to heaven erect uprose,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* For with his eye he could not follow it
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* So as to see aught else than flame alone,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Even as a little cloud ascending upward,
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Thus each along the gorge of the intrenchment
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Was moving; for not one reveals the theft,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And every flame a sinner steals away.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* I stood upon the bridge uprisen to see,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* So that, if I had seized not on a rock,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Down had I fallen without being pushed.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And the Leader, who beheld me so attent,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Exclaimed: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Within the fires the spirits are;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Each swathes himself with that wherewith he burns.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master,”\zsay-e\* I replied, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“by hearing thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I am more sure; but I surmised already\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*It might be so, and already wished to ask thee\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who is within that fire, which comes so cleft\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*At top, it seems uprising from the pyre\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Where was Eteocles with his brother placed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* He answered me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Within there are tormented\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ulysses and Diomed, and thus together\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*They unto vengeance run as unto wrath.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And there within their flame do they lament\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The ambush of the horse, which made the door\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whence issued forth the Romans’ gentle seed;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therein is wept the craft, for which being dead\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Deidamia still deplores Achilles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And pain for the Palladium there is borne.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If they within those sparks possess the power\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To speak,”\zsay-e\* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thee, Master, much I pray,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And re-pray, that the prayer be worth a thousand,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That thou make no denial of awaiting\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Until the horned flame shall hither come;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thou seest that with desire I lean towards it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Worthy is thy entreaty\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of much applause, and therefore I accept it;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But take heed that thy tongue restrain itself.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Leave me to speak, because I have conceived\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That which thou wishest; for they might disdain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Perchance, since they were Greeks, discourse of thine.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When now the flame had come unto that point,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Where to my Leader it seemed time and place,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* After this fashion did I hear him speak:
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O ye, who are twofold within one fire,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If I deserved of you, while I was living,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If I deserved of you or much or little\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When in the world I wrote the lofty verses,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Do not move on, but one of you declare\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Whither, being lost, he went away to die.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Then of the antique flame the greater horn,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Murmuring, began to wave itself about
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Even as a flame doth which the wind fatigues.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Thereafterward, the summit to and fro
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Moving as if it were the tongue that spake,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* It uttered forth a voice, and said: \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*“When I\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*From Circe had departed, who concealed me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*More than a year there near unto Gaeta,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Or ever yet Aeneas named it so,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Nor fondness for my son, nor reverence\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*For my old father, nor the due affection\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Which joyous should have made Penelope,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Could overcome within me the desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*I had to be experienced of the world,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And of the vice and virtue of mankind;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*But I put forth on the high open sea\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*With one sole ship, and that small company\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*By which I never had deserted been.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Both of the shores I saw as far as Spain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Far as Morocco, and the isle of Sardes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And the others which that sea bathes round about.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*I and my company were old and slow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*When at that narrow passage we arrived\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Where Hercules his landmarks set as signals,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*That man no farther onward should adventure.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*On the right hand behind me left I Seville,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And on the other already had left Ceuta.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*‘O brothers, who amid a hundred thousand\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Perils,’ I said, ‘have come unto the West,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*To this so inconsiderable vigil\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Which is remaining of your senses still\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Be ye unwilling to deny the knowledge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Following the sun, of the unpeopled world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Consider ye the seed from which ye sprang;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Ye were not made to live like unto brutes,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*But for pursuit of virtue and of knowledge.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*So eager did I render my companions,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*With this brief exhortation, for the voyage,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*That then I hardly could have held them back.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And having turned our stern unto the morning,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*We of the oars made wings for our mad flight,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Evermore gaining on the larboard side.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Already all the stars of the other pole\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*The night beheld, and ours so very low\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*It did not rise above the ocean floor.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Five times rekindled and as many quenched\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Had been the splendour underneath the moon,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Since we had entered into the deep pass,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*When there appeared to us a mountain, dim\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*From distance, and it seemed to me so high\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*As I had never any one beheld.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Joyful were we, and soon it turned to weeping;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*For out of the new land a whirlwind rose,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And smote upon the fore part of the ship.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Three times it made her whirl with all the waters,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*At the fourth time it made the stern uplift,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*And the prow downward go, as pleased Another,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ulysses"\*Until the sea above us closed again.”\zsay-e\*
\c 27
\cp 27
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Already was the flame erect and quiet,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* To speak no more, and now departed from us
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* With the permission of the gentle Poet;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* When yet another, which behind it came,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Caused us to turn our eyes upon its top
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* By a confused sound that issued from it.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* As the Sicilian bull (that bellowed first
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* With the lament of him, and that was right,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Who with his file had modulated it)
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Bellowed so with the voice of the afflicted,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That, notwithstanding it was made of brass,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* Still it appeared with agony transfixed;
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Thus, by not having any way or issue
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* At first from out the fire, to its own language
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Converted were the melancholy words.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* But afterwards, when they had gathered way
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Up through the point, giving it that vibration
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The tongue had given them in their passage out,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* We heard it said: \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*“O thou, at whom I aim\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*My voice, and who but now wast speaking Lombard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Saying, ‘Now go thy way, no more I urge thee,’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Because I come perchance a little late,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*To stay and speak with me let it not irk thee;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Thou seest it irks not me, and I am burning.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*If thou but lately into this blind world\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Hast fallen down from that sweet Latian land,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Wherefrom I bring the whole of my transgression,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Say, if the Romagnuols have peace or war,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*For I was from the mountains there between\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Urbino and the yoke whence Tiber bursts.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* I still was downward bent and listening,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* When my Conductor touched me on the side,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Speak thou: this one a Latian is.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* And I, who had beforehand my reply
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* In readiness, forthwith began to speak:
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O soul, that down below there art concealed,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Romagna thine is not and never has been\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Without war in the bosom of its tyrants;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But open war I none have left there now.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Ravenna stands as it long years has stood;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The Eagle of Polenta there is brooding,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So that she covers Cervia with her vans.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The city which once made the long resistance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And of the French a sanguinary heap,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Beneath the Green Paws finds itself again;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Verrucchio’s ancient Mastiff and the new,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who made such bad disposal of Montagna,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Where they are wont make wimbles of their teeth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The cities of Lamone and Santerno\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Governs the Lioncel of the white lair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who changes sides ’twixt summer-time and winter;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And that of which the Savio bathes the flank,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Even as it lies between the plain and mountain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Lives between tyranny and a free state.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Now I entreat thee tell us who thou art;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Be not more stubborn than the rest have been,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So may thy name hold front there in the world.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* After the fire a little more had roared
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* In its own fashion, the sharp point it moved
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* This way and that, and then gave forth such breath:
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*“If I believed that my reply were made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*To one who to the world would e’er return,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*This flame without more flickering would stand still;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*But inasmuch as never from this depth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Did any one return, if I hear true,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Without the fear of infamy I answer,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*I was a man of arms, then Cordelier,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Believing thus begirt to make amends;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And truly my belief had been fulfilled\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*But for the High Priest, whom may ill betide,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Who put me back into my former sins;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And how and wherefore I will have thee hear.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*While I was still the form of bone and pulp\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*My mother gave to me, the deeds I did\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Were not those of a lion, but a fox.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*The machinations and the covert ways\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*I knew them all, and practised so their craft,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*That to the ends of earth the sound went forth.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*When now unto that portion of mine age\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*I saw myself arrived, when each one ought\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*To lower the sails, and coil away the ropes,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*That which before had pleased me then displeased me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And penitent and confessing I surrendered,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Ah woe is me! and it would have bestead me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*The Leader of the modern Pharisees\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Having a war near unto Lateran,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And not with Saracens nor with the Jews,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*For each one of his enemies was Christian,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And none of them had been to conquer Acre,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Nor merchandising in the Sultan’s land,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Nor the high office, nor the sacred orders,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*In him regarded, nor in me that cord\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Which used to make those girt with it more meagre;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*But even as Constantine sought out Sylvester\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*To cure his leprosy, within Soracte,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*So this one sought me out as an adept\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*To cure him of the fever of his pride.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Counsel he asked of me, and I was silent,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Because his words appeared inebriate.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And then he said: ‘Be not thy heart afraid;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Henceforth I thee absolve; and thou instruct me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*How to raze Palestrina to the ground.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Heaven have I power to lock and to unlock,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*As thou dost know; therefore the keys are two,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*The which my predecessor held not dear.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Then urged me on his weighty arguments\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*There, where my silence was the worst advice;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And said I: ‘Father, since thou washest me\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Of that sin into which I now must fall,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*The promise long with the fulfilment short\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Will make thee triumph in thy lofty seat.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Francis came afterward, when I was dead,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*For me; but one of the black Cherubim\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Said to him: ‘Take him not; do me no wrong;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*He must come down among my servitors,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Because he gave the fraudulent advice\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*From which time forth I have been at his hair;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*For who repents not cannot be absolved,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Nor can one both repent and will at once,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Because of the contradiction which consents not.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*O miserable me! how I did shudder\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*When he seized on me, saying: ‘Peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Thou didst not think that I was a logician!’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*He bore me unto Minos, who entwined\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Eight times his tail about his stubborn back,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And after he had bitten it in great rage,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Said: ‘Of the thievish fire a culprit this;’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*Wherefore, here where thou seest, am I lost,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Guido da Montefeltro"\*And vested thus in going I bemoan me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* When it had thus completed its recital,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* The flame departed uttering lamentations,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Writhing and flapping its sharp-pointed horn.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Onward we passed, both I and my Conductor,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Up o’er the crag above another arch,
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Which the moat covers, where is paid the fee
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* By those who, sowing discord, win their burden.
\c 28
\cp 28
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* Who ever could, e’en with untrammelled words,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* Tell of the blood and of the wounds in full
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Which now I saw, by many times narrating?
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Each tongue would for a certainty fall short
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* By reason of our speech and memory,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* That have small room to comprehend so much.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* If were again assembled all the people
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Which formerly upon the fateful land
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Of Puglia were lamenting for their blood
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Shed by the Romans and the lingering war
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* That of the rings made such illustrious spoils,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* As Livy has recorded, who errs not,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* With those who felt the agony of blows
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* By making counterstand to Robert Guiscard,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* And all the rest, whose bones are gathered still
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* At Ceperano, where a renegade
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Was each Apulian, and at Tagliacozzo,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* Where without arms the old Alardo conquered,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* And one his limb transpierced, and one lopped off,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Should show, it would be nothing to compare
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* With the disgusting mode of the ninth Bolgia.
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* A cask by losing centre-piece or cant
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Was never shattered so, as I saw one
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Rent from the chin to where one breaketh wind.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* Between his legs were hanging down his entrails;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* His heart was visible, and the dismal sack
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That maketh excrement of what is eaten.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* While I was all absorbed in seeing him,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* He looked at me, and opened with his hands
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* His bosom, saying: \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*“See now how I rend me;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*How mutilated, see, is Mahomet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*In front of me doth Ali weeping go,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Cleft in the face from forelock unto chin;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*And all the others whom thou here beholdest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Disseminators of scandal and of schism\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*While living were, and therefore are cleft thus.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*A devil is behind here, who doth cleave us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Thus cruelly, unto the falchion’s edge\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Putting again each one of all this ream,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*When we have gone around the doleful road;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*By reason that our wounds are closed again\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Ere any one in front of him repass.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*But who art thou, that musest on the crag,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Perchance to postpone going to the pain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*That is adjudged upon thine accusations?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Nor death hath reached him yet, nor guilt doth bring him,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* My Master made reply, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“to be tormented;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But to procure him full experience,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Me, who am dead, behoves it to conduct him\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Down here through Hell, from circle unto circle;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And this is true as that I speak to thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* More than a hundred were there when they heard him,
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Who in the moat stood still to look at me,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Through wonderment oblivious of their torture.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*“Now say to Fra Dolcino, then, to arm him,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Thou, who perhaps wilt shortly see the sun,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*If soon he wish not here to follow me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*So with provisions, that no stress of snow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*May give the victory to the Novarese,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Muhammad"\*Which otherwise to gain would not be easy.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* After one foot to go away he lifted,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* This word did Mahomet say unto me,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Then to depart upon the ground he stretched it.
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Another one, who had his throat pierced through,
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* And nose cut off close underneath the brows,
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* And had no longer but a single ear,
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* Staying to look in wonder with the others,
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Before the others did his gullet open,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Which outwardly was red in every part,
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*“O thou, whom guilt doth not condemn,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*And whom I once saw up in Latian land,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Unless too great similitude deceive me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Call to remembrance Pier da Medicina,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*If e’er thou see again the lovely plain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*That from Vercelli slopes to Marcabo,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*And make it known to the best two of Fano,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*To Messer Guido and Angiolello likewise,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*That if foreseeing here be not in vain,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Cast over from their vessel shall they be,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*And drowned near unto the Cattolica,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*By the betrayal of a tyrant fell.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Between the isles of Cyprus and Majorca\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Neptune ne’er yet beheld so great a crime,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Neither of pirates nor Argolic people.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*That traitor, who sees only with one eye,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*And holds the land, which some one here with me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Would fain be fasting from the vision of,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Will make them come unto a parley with him;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Then will do so, that to Focara’s wind\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*They will not stand in need of vow or prayer.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Show to me and declare,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If thou wouldst have me bear up news of thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Who is this person of the bitter vision.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Then did he lay his hand upon the jaw
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* Of one of his companions, and his mouth
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Oped, crying: \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*“This is he, and he speaks not.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*This one, being banished, every doubt submerged\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*In Caesar by affirming the forearmed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Pier da Medicina"\*Always with detriment allowed delay.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* O how bewildered unto me appeared,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* With tongue asunder in his windpipe slit,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Curio, who in speaking was so bold!
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* And one, who both his hands dissevered had,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* The stumps uplifting through the murky air,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* So that the blood made horrible his face,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Cried out: \zsay-s |who="Mosca dei Lamberti"\*“Thou shalt remember Mosca also,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mosca dei Lamberti"\*Who said, alas! ‘A thing done has an end!’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Mosca dei Lamberti"\*Which was an ill seed for the Tuscan people.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“And death unto thy race,”\zsay-e\* thereto I added;
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Whence he, accumulating woe on woe,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* Departed, like a person sad and crazed.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* But I remained to look upon the crowd;
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And saw a thing which I should be afraid,
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Without some further proof, even to recount,
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* If it were not that conscience reassures me,
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* That good companion which emboldens man
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* Beneath the hauberk of its feeling pure.
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* I truly saw, and still I seem to see it,
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* A trunk without a head walk in like manner
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* As walked the others of the mournful herd.
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And by the hair it held the head dissevered,
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* Hung from the hand in fashion of a lantern,
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* And that upon us gazed and said: \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*“O me!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* It of itself made to itself a lamp,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* And they were two in one, and one in two;
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* How that can be, He knows who so ordains it.
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* When it was come close to the bridge’s foot,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* It lifted high its arm with all the head,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* To bring more closely unto us its words,
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Which were: \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*“Behold now the sore penalty,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Thou, who dost breathing go the dead beholding;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Behold if any be as great as this.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*And so that thou may carry news of me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Know that Bertram de Born am I, the same\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Who gave to the Young King the evil comfort.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*I made the father and the son rebellious;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Achitophel not more with Absalom\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*And David did with his accursed goadings.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Because I parted persons so united,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Parted do I now bear my brain, alas!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*From its beginning, which is in this trunk.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bertran de Born"\*Thus is observed in me the counterpoise.”\zsay-e\*
\c 29
\cp 29
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* The many people and the divers wounds
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* These eyes of mine had so inebriated,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* That they were wishful to stand still and weep;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* But said Virgilius: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“What dost thou still gaze at?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Why is thy sight still riveted down there\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among the mournful, mutilated shades?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou hast not done so at the other Bolge;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Consider, if to count them thou believest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That two-and-twenty miles the valley winds,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And now the moon is underneath our feet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Henceforth the time allotted us is brief,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And more is to be seen than what thou seest.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If thou hadst,”\zsay-e\* I made answer thereupon,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Attended to the cause for which I looked,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Perhaps a longer stay thou wouldst have pardoned.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Meanwhile my Guide departed, and behind him
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* I went, already making my reply,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And superadding: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“In that cavern where\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I held mine eyes with such attention fixed,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I think a spirit of my blood laments\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*The sin which down below there costs so much.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Then said the Master: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Be no longer broken\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thy thought from this time forward upon him;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Attend elsewhere, and there let him remain;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For him I saw below the little bridge,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Pointing at thee, and threatening with his finger\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Fiercely, and heard him called Geri del Bello.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*So wholly at that time wast thou impeded\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*By him who formerly held Altaforte,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou didst not look that way; so he departed.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O my Conductor, his own violent death,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Which is not yet avenged for him,”\zsay-e\* I said,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“By any who is sharer in the shame,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Made him disdainful; whence he went away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*As I imagine, without speaking to me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And thereby made me pity him the more.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Thus did we speak as far as the first place
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* Upon the crag, which the next valley shows
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Down to the bottom, if there were more light.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* When we were now right over the last cloister
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Of Malebolge, so that its lay-brothers
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* Could manifest themselves unto our sight,
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* Divers lamentings pierced me through and through,
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* Which with compassion had their arrows barbed,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Whereat mine ears I covered with my hands.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* What pain would be, if from the hospitals
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Of Valdichiana, ’twixt July and September,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* And of Maremma and Sardinia
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* All the diseases in one moat were gathered,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Such was it here, and such a stench came from it
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* As from putrescent limbs is wont to issue.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* We had descended on the furthest bank
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* From the long crag, upon the left hand still,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* And then more vivid was my power of sight
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Down tow’rds the bottom, where the ministress
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Of the high Lord, Justice infallible,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* Punishes forgers, which she here records.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* I do not think a sadder sight to see
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Was in Aegina the whole people sick,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* (When was the air so full of pestilence,
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* The animals, down to the little worm,
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* All fell, and afterwards the ancient people,
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* According as the poets have affirmed,
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Were from the seed of ants restored again,)
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* Than was it to behold through that dark valley
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* The spirits languishing in divers heaps.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* This on the belly, that upon the back
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* One of the other lay, and others crawling
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* Shifted themselves along the dismal road.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* We step by step went onward without speech,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Gazing upon and listening to the sick
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* Who had not strength enough to lift their bodies.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* I saw two sitting leaned against each other,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* As leans in heating platter against platter,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* From head to foot bespotted o’er with scabs;
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* And never saw I plied a currycomb
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* By stable-boy for whom his master waits,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Or him who keeps awake unwillingly,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* As every one was plying fast the bite
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of nails upon himself, for the great rage
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Of itching which no other succour had.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And the nails downward with them dragged the scab,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* In fashion as a knife the scales of bream,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* Or any other fish that has them largest.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O thou, that with thy fingers dost dismail thee,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Began my Leader unto one of them,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“And makest of them pincers now and then,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Tell me if any Latian is with those\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who are herein; so may thy nails suffice thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To all eternity unto this work.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“Latians are we, whom thou so wasted seest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Both of us here,”\zsay-e\* one weeping made reply;
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“But who art thou, that questionest about us?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And said the Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“One am I who descends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Down with this living man from cliff to cliff,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And I intend to show Hell unto him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Then broken was their mutual support,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* And trembling each one turned himself to me,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* With others who had heard him by rebound.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Wholly to me did the good Master gather,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Say unto them whate’er thou wishest.”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* And I began, since he would have it so:
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“So may your memory not steal away\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In the first world from out the minds of men,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*But so may it survive ’neath many suns,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Say to me who ye are, and of what people;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Let not your foul and loathsome punishment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Make you afraid to show yourselves to me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“I of Arezzo was,”\zsay-e\* one made reply,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“And Albert of Siena had me burned;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*But what I died for does not bring me here.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*’Tis true I said to him, speaking in jest,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*That I could rise by flight into the air,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*And he who had conceit, but little wit,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Would have me show to him the art; and only\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Because no Daedalus I made him, made me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Be burned by one who held him as his son.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*But unto the last Bolgia of the ten,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*For alchemy, which in the world I practised,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Minos, who cannot err, has me condemned.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* And to the Poet said I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Now was ever\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*So vain a people as the Sienese?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Not for a certainty the French by far.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Whereat the other leper, who had heard me,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* Replied unto my speech: \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*“Taking out Stricca,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Who knew the art of moderate expenses,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*And Niccolo, who the luxurious use\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Of cloves discovered earliest of all\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Within that garden where such seed takes root;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*And taking out the band, among whom squandered\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Caccia d’Ascian his vineyards and vast woods,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*And where his wit the Abbagliato proffered!\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*But, that thou know who thus doth second thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Against the Sienese, make sharp thine eye\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Tow’rds me, so that my face well answer thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*And thou shalt see I am Capocchio’s shade,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Who metals falsified by alchemy;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*Thou must remember, if I well descry thee,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Capocchio"\*How I a skilful ape of nature was.”\zsay-e\*
\c 30
\cp 30
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* ’Twas at the time when Juno was enraged,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* For Semele, against the Theban blood,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* As she already more than once had shown,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* So reft of reason Athamas became,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* That, seeing his own wife with children twain
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Walking encumbered upon either hand,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* He cried: \zsay-s |who="Athamas"\*“Spread out the nets, that I may take\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Athamas"\*The lioness and her whelps upon the passage;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* And then extended his unpitying claws,
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Seizing the first, who had the name Learchus,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* And whirled him round, and dashed him on a rock;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And she, with the other burthen, drowned herself;—
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* And at the time when fortune downward hurled
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* The Trojan’s arrogance, that all things dared,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* So that the king was with his kingdom crushed,
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* Hecuba sad, disconsolate, and captive,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* When lifeless she beheld Polyxena,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And of her Polydorus on the shore
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Of ocean was the dolorous one aware,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Out of her senses like a dog she barked,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* So much the anguish had her mind distorted;
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* But not of Thebes the furies nor the Trojan
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Were ever seen in any one so cruel
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* In goading beasts, and much more human members,
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* As I beheld two shadows pale and naked,
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Who, biting, in the manner ran along
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* That a boar does, when from the sty turned loose.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* One to Capocchio came, and by the nape
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Seized with its teeth his neck, so that in dragging
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* It made his belly grate the solid bottom.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And the Aretine, who trembling had remained,
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Said to me: \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“That mad sprite is Gianni Schicchi,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*And raving goes thus harrying other people.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O,”\zsay-e\* said I to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“so may not the other\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Set teeth on thee, let it not weary thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*To tell us who it is, ere it dart hence.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*“That is the ancient ghost\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Of the nefarious Myrrha, who became\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Beyond all rightful love her father’s lover.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*She came to sin with him after this manner,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*By counterfeiting of another’s form;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*As he who goeth yonder undertook,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*That he might gain the lady of the herd,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*To counterfeit in himself Buoso Donati,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Griffolino"\*Making a will and giving it due form.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And after the two maniacs had passed
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* On whom I held mine eye, I turned it back
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* To look upon the other evil-born.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* I saw one made in fashion of a lute,
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* If he had only had the groin cut off
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Just at the point at which a man is forked.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* The heavy dropsy, that so disproportions
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* The limbs with humours, which it ill concocts,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* That the face corresponds not to the belly,
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* Compelled him so to hold his lips apart
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* As does the hectic, who because of thirst
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* One tow’rds the chin, the other upward turns.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“O ye, who without any torment are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*And why I know not, in the world of woe,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* He said to us, \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“behold, and be attentive\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Unto the misery of Master Adam;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*I had while living much of what I wished,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*And now, alas! a drop of water crave.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*The rivulets, that from the verdant hills\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Of Cassentin descend down into Arno,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Making their channels to be cold and moist,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Ever before me stand, and not in vain;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*For far more doth their image dry me up\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Than the disease which strips my face of flesh.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*The rigid justice that chastises me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Draweth occasion from the place in which\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*I sinned, to put the more my sighs in flight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*There is Romena, where I counterfeited\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*The currency imprinted with the Baptist,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*For which I left my body burned above.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*But if I here could see the tristful soul\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Of Guido, or Alessandro, or their brother,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*For Branda’s fount I would not give the sight.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*One is within already, if the raving\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Shades that are going round about speak truth;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*But what avails it me, whose limbs are tied?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*If I were only still so light, that in\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*A hundred years I could advance one inch,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*I had already started on the way,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Seeking him out among this squalid folk,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Although the circuit be eleven miles,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*And be not less than half a mile across.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*For them am I in such a family;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*They did induce me into coining florins,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Which had three carats of impurity.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Who are the two poor wretches\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That smoke like unto a wet hand in winter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Lying there close upon thy right-hand confines?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“I found them here,”\zsay-e\* replied he, \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“when I rained\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Into this chasm, and since they have not turned,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Nor do I think they will for evermore.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*One the false woman is who accused Joseph,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*The other the false Sinon, Greek of Troy;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*From acute fever they send forth such reek.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And one of them, who felt himself annoyed
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* At being, peradventure, named so darkly,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Smote with the fist upon his hardened paunch.
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* It gave a sound, as if it were a drum;
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And Master Adam smote him in the face,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* With arm that did not seem to be less hard,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Saying to him: \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“Although be taken from me\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*All motion, for my limbs that heavy are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*I have an arm unfettered for such need.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Whereat he answer made: \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*“When thou didst go\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*Unto the fire, thou hadst it not so ready:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*But hadst it so and more when thou wast coining.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* The dropsical: \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“Thou sayest true in that;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*But thou wast not so true a witness there,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Where thou wast questioned of the truth at Troy.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*“If I spake false, thou falsifiedst the coin,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* Said Sinon; \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*“and for one fault I am here,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*And thou for more than any other demon.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“Remember, perjurer, about the horse,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* He made reply who had the swollen belly,
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“And rueful be it thee the whole world knows it.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*“Rueful to thee the thirst be wherewith cracks\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*Thy tongue,”\zsay-e\* the Greek said, \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*“and the putrid water\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Sinon"\*That hedges so thy paunch before thine eyes.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Then the false-coiner: \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*“So is gaping wide\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Thy mouth for speaking evil, as ’tis wont;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Because if I have thirst, and humour stuff me\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Thou hast the burning and the head that aches,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*And to lick up the mirror of Narcissus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Master Adam"\*Thou wouldst not want words many to invite thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* In listening to them was I wholly fixed,
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* When said the Master to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Now just look,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For little wants it that I quarrel with thee.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* When him I heard in anger speak to me,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* I turned me round towards him with such shame
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* That still it eddies through my memory.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* And as he is who dreams of his own harm,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Who dreaming wishes it may be a dream,
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* So that he craves what is, as if it were not;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Such I became, not having power to speak,
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* For to excuse myself I wished, and still
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* Excused myself, and did not think I did it.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Less shame doth wash away a greater fault,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* The Master said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“than this of thine has been;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore thyself disburden of all sadness,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And make account that I am aye beside thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If e’er it come to pass that fortune bring thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where there are people in a like dispute;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For a base wish it is to wish to hear it.”\zsay-e\*
\c 31
\cp 31
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* One and the selfsame tongue first wounded me,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* So that it tinged the one cheek and the other,
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* And then held out to me the medicine;
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Thus do I hear that once Achilles’ spear,
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* His and his father’s, used to be the cause
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* First of a sad and then a gracious boon.
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* We turned our backs upon the wretched valley,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* Upon the bank that girds it round about,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Going across it without any speech.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* There it was less than night, and less than day,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* So that my sight went little in advance;
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* But I could hear the blare of a loud horn,
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* So loud it would have made each thunder faint,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Which, counter to it following its way,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Mine eyes directed wholly to one place.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* After the dolorous discomfiture
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* When Charlemagne the holy emprise lost,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* So terribly Orlando sounded not.
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* Short while my head turned thitherward I held
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* When many lofty towers I seemed to see,
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* Whereat I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Master, say, what town is this?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Because thou peerest forth\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Athwart the darkness at too great a distance,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*It happens that thou errest in thy fancy.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Well shalt thou see, if thou arrivest there,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*How much the sense deceives itself by distance;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore a little faster spur thee on.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* Then tenderly he took me by the hand,
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Before we farther have advanced,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That the reality may seem to thee\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Less strange, know that these are not towers, but giants,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And they are in the well, around the bank,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*From navel downward, one and all of them.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* As, when the fog is vanishing away,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Little by little doth the sight refigure
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Whate’er the mist that crowds the air conceals,
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* So, piercing through the dense and darksome air,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* More and more near approaching tow’rd the verge,
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* My error fled, and fear came over me;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Because as on its circular parapets
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Montereggione crowns itself with towers,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* E’en thus the margin which surrounds the well
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* With one half of their bodies turreted
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* The horrible giants, whom Jove menaces
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* E’en now from out the heavens when he thunders.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* And I of one already saw the face,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Shoulders, and breast, and great part of the belly,
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* And down along his sides both of the arms.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Certainly Nature, when she left the making
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Of animals like these, did well indeed,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* By taking such executors from Mars;
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And if of elephants and whales she doth not
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Repent her, whosoever looketh subtly
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* More just and more discreet will hold her for it;
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* For where the argument of intellect
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* Is added unto evil will and power,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* No rampart can the people make against it.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* His face appeared to me as long and large
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* As is at Rome the pine-cone of Saint Peter’s,
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* And in proportion were the other bones;
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* So that the margin, which an apron was
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* Down from the middle, showed so much of him
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* Above it, that to reach up to his hair
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* Three Frieslanders in vain had vaunted them;
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* For I beheld thirty great palms of him
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* Down from the place where man his mantle buckles.
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Nimrod"\*“Raphael mai amech izabi almi,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* Began to clamour the ferocious mouth,
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* To which were not befitting sweeter psalms.
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* And unto him my Guide: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Soul idiotic,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Keep to thy horn, and vent thyself with that,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When wrath or other passion touches thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Search round thy neck, and thou wilt find the belt\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which keeps it fastened, O bewildered soul,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And see it, where it bars thy mighty breast.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Then said to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“He doth himself accuse;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This one is Nimrod, by whose evil thought\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*One language in the world is not still used.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here let us leave him and not speak in vain;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For even such to him is every language\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*As his to others, which to none is known.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Therefore a longer journey did we make,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* Turned to the left, and a crossbow-shot off
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* We found another far more fierce and large.
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* In binding him, who might the master be
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* I cannot say; but he had pinioned close
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Behind the right arm, and in front the other,
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* With chains, that held him so begirt about
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* From the neck down, that on the part uncovered
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* It wound itself as far as the fifth gyre.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“This proud one wished to make experiment\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of his own power against the Supreme Jove,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* My Leader said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“whence he has such a guerdon.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Ephialtes is his name; he showed great prowess.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*What time the giants terrified the gods;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The arms he wielded never more he moves.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* And I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If possible, I should wish\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That of the measureless Briareus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*These eyes of mine might have experience.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Whence he replied: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou shalt behold Antaeus\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Close by here, who can speak and is unbound,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Who at the bottom of all crime shall place us.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Much farther yon is he whom thou wouldst see,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And he is bound, and fashioned like to this one,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Save that he seems in aspect more ferocious.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* There never was an earthquake of such might
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* That it could shake a tower so violently,
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* As Ephialtes suddenly shook himself.
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* Then was I more afraid of death than ever,
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* For nothing more was needful than the fear,
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* If I had not beheld the manacles.
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* Then we proceeded farther in advance,
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* And to Antaeus came, who, full five ells
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* Without the head, forth issued from the cavern.
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“O thou, who in the valley fortunate,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which Scipio the heir of glory made,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When Hannibal turned back with all his hosts,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Once brought’st a thousand lions for thy prey,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And who, hadst thou been at the mighty war\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Among thy brothers, some it seems still think\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The sons of Earth the victory would have gained:\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Place us below, nor be disdainful of it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*There where the cold doth lock Cocytus up.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Make us not go to Tityus nor Typhoeus;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*This one can give of that which here is longed for;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Therefore stoop down, and do not curl thy lip.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still in the world can he restore thy fame;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Because he lives, and still expects long life,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*If to itself Grace call him not untimely.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* So said the Master; and in haste the other
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* His hands extended and took up my Guide,—
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Hands whose great pressure Hercules once felt.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* Virgilius, when he felt himself embraced,
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Said unto me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Draw nigh, that I may take thee;”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* Then of himself and me one bundle made.
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* As seems the Carisenda, to behold
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Beneath the leaning side, when goes a cloud
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Above it so that opposite it hangs;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Such did Antaeus seem to me, who stood
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* Watching to see him stoop, and then it was
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* I could have wished to go some other way.
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* But lightly in the abyss, which swallows up
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* Judas with Lucifer, he put us down;
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* Nor thus bowed downward made he there delay,
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* But, as a mast does in a ship, uprose.
\c 32
\cp 32
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* If I had rhymes both rough and stridulous,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* As were appropriate to the dismal hole
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Down upon which thrust all the other rocks,
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* I would press out the juice of my conception
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* More fully; but because I have them not,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Not without fear I bring myself to speak;
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* For ’tis no enterprise to take in jest,
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* To sketch the bottom of all the universe,
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* Nor for a tongue that cries Mamma and Babbo.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* But may those Ladies help this verse of mine,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* Who helped Amphion in enclosing Thebes,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* That from the fact the word be not diverse.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* O rabble ill-begotten above all,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* Who’re in the place to speak of which is hard,
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* ’Twere better ye had here been sheep or goats!
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* When we were down within the darksome well,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* Beneath the giant’s feet, but lower far,
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* And I was scanning still the lofty wall,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* I heard it said to me: \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*“Look how thou steppest!\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*Take heed thou do not trample with thy feet\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="A voice"\*The heads of the tired, miserable brothers!”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* Whereat I turned me round, and saw before me
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* And underfoot a lake, that from the frost
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* The semblance had of glass, and not of water.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* So thick a veil ne’er made upon its current
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* In winter-time Danube in Austria,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* Nor there beneath the frigid sky the Don,
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* As there was here; so that if Tambernich
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* Had fallen upon it, or Pietrapana,
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* E’en at the edge ’twould not have given a creak.
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* And as to croak the frog doth place himself
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* With muzzle out of water,—when is dreaming
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Of gleaning oftentimes the peasant-girl,—
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Livid, as far down as where shame appears,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* Were the disconsolate shades within the ice,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Setting their teeth unto the note of storks.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* Each one his countenance held downward bent;
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* From mouth the cold, from eyes the doleful heart
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* Among them witness of itself procures.
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* When round about me somewhat I had looked,
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* I downward turned me, and saw two so close,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* The hair upon their heads together mingled.
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ye who so strain your breasts together, tell me,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* I said, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“who are you;”\zsay-e\* and they bent their necks,
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* And when to me their faces they had lifted,
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Their eyes, which first were only moist within,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Gushed o’er the eyelids, and the frost congealed
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* The tears between, and locked them up again.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* Clamp never bound together wood with wood
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* So strongly; whereat they, like two he-goats,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* Butted together, so much wrath o’ercame them.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* And one, who had by reason of the cold
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* Lost both his ears, still with his visage downward,
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Said: \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*“Why dost thou so mirror thyself in us?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*If thou desire to know who these two are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*The valley whence Bisenzio descends\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Belonged to them and to their father Albert.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*They from one body came, and all Caina\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Thou shalt search through, and shalt not find a shade\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*More worthy to be fixed in gelatine;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Not he in whom were broken breast and shadow\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*At one and the same blow by Arthur’s hand;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Focaccia not; not he who me encumbers\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*So with his head I see no farther forward,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*And bore the name of Sassol Mascheroni;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Well knowest thou who he was, if thou art Tuscan.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*And that thou put me not to further speech,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*Know that I Camicion de’ Pazzi was,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Camicion de' Pazzi"\*And wait Carlino to exonerate me.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* Then I beheld a thousand faces, made
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* Purple with cold; whence o’er me comes a shudder,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And evermore will come, at frozen ponds.
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* And while we were advancing tow’rds the middle,
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* Where everything of weight unites together,
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* And I was shivering in the eternal shade,
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* Whether ’twere will, or destiny, or chance,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* I know not; but in walking ’mong the heads
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* I struck my foot hard in the face of one.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Weeping he growled: \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“Why dost thou trample me?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Unless thou comest to increase the vengeance\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*of Montaperti, why dost thou molest me?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* And I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, now wait here for me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That I through him may issue from a doubt;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Then thou mayst hurry me, as thou shalt wish.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* The Leader stopped; and to that one I said
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Who was blaspheming vehemently still:
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Who art thou, that thus reprehendest others?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“Now who art thou, that goest through Antenora\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Smiting,”\zsay-e\* replied he, \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“other people’s cheeks,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*So that, if thou wert living, ’twere too much?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Living I am, and dear to thee it may be,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Was my response, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“if thou demandest fame,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That ’mid the other notes thy name I place.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“For the reverse I long;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Take thyself hence, and give me no more trouble;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*For ill thou knowest to flatter in this hollow.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Then by the scalp behind I seized upon him,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* And said: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“It must needs be thou name thyself,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Or not a hair remain upon thee here.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* Whence he to me: \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“Though thou strip off my hair,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*I will not tell thee who I am, nor show thee,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*If on my head a thousand times thou fall.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* I had his hair in hand already twisted,
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* And more than one shock of it had pulled out,
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* He barking, with his eyes held firmly down,
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* When cried another: \zsay-s |who="Buoso da Duera"\*“What doth ail thee, Bocca?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buoso da Duera"\*Is’t not enough to clatter with thy jaws,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Buoso da Duera"\*But thou must bark? what devil touches thee?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Now,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I care not to have thee speak,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Accursed traitor; for unto thy shame\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I will report of thee veracious news.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“Begone,”\zsay-e\* replied he, \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*“and tell what thou wilt,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*But be not silent, if thou issue hence,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Of him who had just now his tongue so prompt;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*He weepeth here the silver of the French;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*‘I saw,’ thus canst thou phrase it, ‘him of Duera\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*There where the sinners stand out in the cold.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*If thou shouldst questioned be who else was there,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Thou hast beside thee him of Beccaria,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Of whom the gorget Florence slit asunder;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Gianni del Soldanier, I think, may be\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Yonder with Ganellon, and Tebaldello\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Bocca degli Abati"\*Who oped Faenza when the people slept.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* Already we had gone away from him,
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* When I beheld two frozen in one hole,
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* So that one head a hood was to the other;
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* And even as bread through hunger is devoured,
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* The uppermost on the other set his teeth,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* There where the brain is to the nape united.
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Not in another fashion Tydeus gnawed
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* The temples of Menalippus in disdain,
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* Than that one did the skull and the other things.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O thou, who showest by such bestial sign\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thy hatred against him whom thou art eating,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Tell me the wherefore,”\zsay-e\* said I, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“with this compact,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*That if thou rightfully of him complain,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*In knowing who ye are, and his transgression,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*I in the world above repay thee for it,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*If that wherewith I speak be not dried up.”\zsay-e\*
\c 33
\cp 33
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* His mouth uplifted from his grim repast,
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* That sinner, wiping it upon the hair
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* Of the same head that he behind had wasted.
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* Then he began: \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*“Thou wilt that I renew\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*The desperate grief, which wrings my heart already\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*To think of only, ere I speak of it;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*But if my words be seed that may bear fruit\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Of infamy to the traitor whom I gnaw,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Speaking and weeping shalt thou see together.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*I know not who thou art, nor by what mode\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Thou hast come down here; but a Florentine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Thou seemest to me truly, when I hear thee.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Thou hast to know I was Count Ugolino,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And this one was Ruggieri the Archbishop;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Now I will tell thee why I am such a neighbour.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*That, by effect of his malicious thoughts,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Trusting in him I was made prisoner,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And after put to death, I need not say;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*But ne’ertheless what thou canst not have heard,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*That is to say, how cruel was my death,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Hear shalt thou, and shalt know if he has wronged me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*A narrow perforation in the mew,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Which bears because of me the title of Famine,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And in which others still must be locked up,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Had shown me through its opening many moons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Already, when I dreamed the evil dream\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Which of the future rent for me the veil.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*This one appeared to me as lord and master,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Hunting the wolf and whelps upon the mountain\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*For which the Pisans cannot Lucca see.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*With sleuth-hounds gaunt, and eager, and well trained,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Gualandi with Sismondi and Lanfranchi\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*He had sent out before him to the front.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*After brief course seemed unto me forespent\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*The father and the sons, and with sharp tushes\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*It seemed to me I saw their flanks ripped open.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*When I before the morrow was awake,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Moaning amid their sleep I heard my sons\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Who with me were, and asking after bread.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Cruel indeed art thou, if yet thou grieve not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Thinking of what my heart foreboded me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And weep’st thou not, what art thou wont to weep at?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*They were awake now, and the hour drew nigh\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*At which our food used to be brought to us,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And through his dream was each one apprehensive;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And I heard locking up the under door\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Of the horrible tower; whereat without a word\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*I gazed into the faces of my sons.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*I wept not, I within so turned to stone;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*They wept; and darling little Anselm mine\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Said: ‘Thou dost gaze so, father, what doth ail thee?’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Still not a tear I shed, nor answer made\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*All of that day, nor yet the night thereafter,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Until another sun rose on the world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*As now a little glimmer made its way\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Into the dolorous prison, and I saw\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Upon four faces my own very aspect,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Both of my hands in agony I bit;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And, thinking that I did it from desire\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Of eating, on a sudden they uprose,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And said they: ‘Father, much less pain ’twill give us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*If thou do eat of us; thyself didst clothe us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*With this poor flesh, and do thou strip it off.’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*I calmed me then, not to make them more sad.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*That day we all were silent, and the next.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Ah! obdurate earth, wherefore didst thou not open?\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*When we had come unto the fourth day, Gaddo\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Threw himself down outstretched before my feet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Saying, ‘My father, why dost thou not help me?’\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And there he died; and, as thou seest me,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*I saw the three fall, one by one, between\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*The fifth day and the sixth; whence I betook me,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Already blind, to groping over each,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*And three days called them after they were dead;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* \zsay-s |who="Ugolino"\*Then hunger did what sorrow could not do.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When he had said this, with his eyes distorted,
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* The wretched skull resumed he with his teeth,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* Which, as a dog’s, upon the bone were strong.
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Ah! Pisa, thou opprobrium of the people
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* Of the fair land there where the ‘Si’ doth sound,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* Since slow to punish thee thy neighbours are,
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* Let the Capraia and Gorgona move,
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* And make a hedge across the mouth of Arno
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* That every person in thee it may drown!
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* For if Count Ugolino had the fame
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* Of having in thy castles thee betrayed,
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Thou shouldst not on such cross have put his sons.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* Guiltless of any crime, thou modern Thebes!
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Their youth made Uguccione and Brigata,
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* And the other two my song doth name above!
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* We passed still farther onward, where the ice
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Another people ruggedly enswathes,
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* Not downward turned, but all of them reversed.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* Weeping itself there does not let them weep,
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* And grief that finds a barrier in the eyes
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* Turns itself inward to increase the anguish;
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* Because the earliest tears a cluster form,
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* And, in the manner of a crystal visor,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* Fill all the cup beneath the eyebrow full.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* And notwithstanding that, as in a callus,
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* Because of cold all sensibility
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* Its station had abandoned in my face,
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* Still it appeared to me I felt some wind;
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* Whence I: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“My Master, who sets this in motion?\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Is not below here every vapour quenched?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* Whence he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Full soon shalt thou be where\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thine eye shall answer make to thee of this,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Seeing the cause which raineth down the blast.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* And one of the wretches of the frozen crust
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* Cried out to us: \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*“O souls so merciless\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*That the last post is given unto you,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Lift from mine eyes the rigid veils, that I\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*May vent the sorrow which impregns my heart\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*A little, ere the weeping recongeal.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* Whence I to him: \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“If thou wouldst have me help thee\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Say who thou wast; and if I free thee not,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*May I go to the bottom of the ice.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* Then he replied: \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*“I am Friar Alberigo;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*He am I of the fruit of the bad garden,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Who here a date am getting for my fig.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“O,”\zsay-e\* said I to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“now art thou, too, dead?”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*“How may my body fare\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Up in the world, no knowledge I possess.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Such an advantage has this Ptolomaea,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*That oftentimes the soul descendeth here\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Sooner than Atropos in motion sets it.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*And, that thou mayest more willingly remove\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*From off my countenance these glassy tears,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Know that as soon as any soul betrays\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*As I have done, his body by a demon\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Is taken from him, who thereafter rules it,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Until his time has wholly been revolved.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Itself down rushes into such a cistern;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*And still perchance above appears the body\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Of yonder shade, that winters here behind me.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*This thou shouldst know, if thou hast just come down;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*It is Ser Branca d’ Oria, and many years\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Have passed away since he was thus locked up.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“I think,”\zsay-e\* said I to him, \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“thou dost deceive me;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 140 \vp 140\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*For Branca d’ Oria is not dead as yet,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 141 \vp 141\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*And eats, and drinks, and sleeps, and puts on clothes.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 142 \vp 142\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*“In moat above,”\zsay-e\* said he, \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*“of Malebranche,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 143 \vp 143\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*There where is boiling the tenacious pitch,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 144 \vp 144\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*As yet had Michel Zanche not arrived,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 145 \vp 145\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*When this one left a devil in his stead\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 146 \vp 146\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*In his own body and one near of kin,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 147 \vp 147\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Who made together with him the betrayal.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 148 \vp 148\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*But hitherward stretch out thy hand forthwith,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 149 \vp 149\vp* \zsay-s |who="Fra Alberigo"\*Open mine eyes;”\zsay-e\*—and open them I did not,
\q1 \v 150 \vp 150\vp* And to be rude to him was courtesy.
\b
\q1 \v 151 \vp 151\vp* Ah, Genoese! ye men at variance
\q1 \v 152 \vp 152\vp* With every virtue, full of every vice
\q1 \v 153 \vp 153\vp* Wherefore are ye not scattered from the world?
\b
\q1 \v 154 \vp 154\vp* For with the vilest spirit of Romagna
\q1 \v 155 \vp 155\vp* I found of you one such, who for his deeds
\q1 \v 156 \vp 156\vp* In soul already in Cocytus bathes,
\b
\q1 \v 157 \vp 157\vp* And still above in body seems alive!
\c 34
\cp 34
\q1 \v 1 \vp 1\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“‘Vexilla Regis prodeunt Inferni’\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 2 \vp 2\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Towards us; therefore look in front of thee,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 3 \vp 3\vp* My Master said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“if thou discernest him.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 4 \vp 4\vp* As, when there breathes a heavy fog, or when
\q1 \v 5 \vp 5\vp* Our hemisphere is darkening into night,
\q1 \v 6 \vp 6\vp* Appears far off a mill the wind is turning,
\b
\q1 \v 7 \vp 7\vp* Methought that such a building then I saw;
\q1 \v 8 \vp 8\vp* And, for the wind, I drew myself behind
\q1 \v 9 \vp 9\vp* My Guide, because there was no other shelter.
\b
\q1 \v 10 \vp 10\vp* Now was I, and with fear in verse I put it,
\q1 \v 11 \vp 11\vp* There where the shades were wholly covered up,
\q1 \v 12 \vp 12\vp* And glimmered through like unto straws in glass.
\b
\q1 \v 13 \vp 13\vp* Some prone are lying, others stand erect,
\q1 \v 14 \vp 14\vp* This with the head, and that one with the soles;
\q1 \v 15 \vp 15\vp* Another, bow-like, face to feet inverts.
\b
\q1 \v 16 \vp 16\vp* When in advance so far we had proceeded,
\q1 \v 17 \vp 17\vp* That it my Master pleased to show to me
\q1 \v 18 \vp 18\vp* The creature who once had the beauteous semblance,
\b
\q1 \v 19 \vp 19\vp* He from before me moved and made me stop,
\q1 \v 20 \vp 20\vp* Saying: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Behold Dis, and behold the place\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 21 \vp 21\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Where thou with fortitude must arm thyself.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 22 \vp 22\vp* How frozen I became and powerless then,
\q1 \v 23 \vp 23\vp* Ask it not, Reader, for I write it not,
\q1 \v 24 \vp 24\vp* Because all language would be insufficient.
\b
\q1 \v 25 \vp 25\vp* I did not die, and I alive remained not;
\q1 \v 26 \vp 26\vp* Think for thyself now, hast thou aught of wit,
\q1 \v 27 \vp 27\vp* What I became, being of both deprived.
\b
\q1 \v 28 \vp 28\vp* The Emperor of the kingdom dolorous
\q1 \v 29 \vp 29\vp* From his mid-breast forth issued from the ice;
\q1 \v 30 \vp 30\vp* And better with a giant I compare
\b
\q1 \v 31 \vp 31\vp* Than do the giants with those arms of his;
\q1 \v 32 \vp 32\vp* Consider now how great must be that whole,
\q1 \v 33 \vp 33\vp* Which unto such a part conforms itself.
\b
\q1 \v 34 \vp 34\vp* Were he as fair once, as he now is foul,
\q1 \v 35 \vp 35\vp* And lifted up his brow against his Maker,
\q1 \v 36 \vp 36\vp* Well may proceed from him all tribulation.
\b
\q1 \v 37 \vp 37\vp* O, what a marvel it appeared to me,
\q1 \v 38 \vp 38\vp* When I beheld three faces on his head!
\q1 \v 39 \vp 39\vp* The one in front, and that vermilion was;
\b
\q1 \v 40 \vp 40\vp* Two were the others, that were joined with this
\q1 \v 41 \vp 41\vp* Above the middle part of either shoulder,
\q1 \v 42 \vp 42\vp* And they were joined together at the crest;
\b
\q1 \v 43 \vp 43\vp* And the right-hand one seemed ’twixt white and yellow;
\q1 \v 44 \vp 44\vp* The left was such to look upon as those
\q1 \v 45 \vp 45\vp* Who come from where the Nile falls valley-ward.
\b
\q1 \v 46 \vp 46\vp* Underneath each came forth two mighty wings,
\q1 \v 47 \vp 47\vp* Such as befitting were so great a bird;
\q1 \v 48 \vp 48\vp* Sails of the sea I never saw so large.
\b
\q1 \v 49 \vp 49\vp* No feathers had they, but as of a bat
\q1 \v 50 \vp 50\vp* Their fashion was; and he was waving them,
\q1 \v 51 \vp 51\vp* So that three winds proceeded forth therefrom.
\b
\q1 \v 52 \vp 52\vp* Thereby Cocytus wholly was congealed.
\q1 \v 53 \vp 53\vp* With six eyes did he weep, and down three chins
\q1 \v 54 \vp 54\vp* Trickled the tear-drops and the bloody drivel.
\b
\q1 \v 55 \vp 55\vp* At every mouth he with his teeth was crunching
\q1 \v 56 \vp 56\vp* A sinner, in the manner of a brake,
\q1 \v 57 \vp 57\vp* So that he three of them tormented thus.
\b
\q1 \v 58 \vp 58\vp* To him in front the biting was as naught
\q1 \v 59 \vp 59\vp* Unto the clawing, for sometimes the spine
\q1 \v 60 \vp 60\vp* Utterly stripped of all the skin remained.
\b
\q1 \v 61 \vp 61\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“That soul up there which has the greatest pain,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 62 \vp 62\vp* The Master said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“is Judas Iscariot;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 63 \vp 63\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*With head inside, he plies his legs without.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 64 \vp 64\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Of the two others, who head downward are,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 65 \vp 65\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The one who hangs from the black jowl is Brutus;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 66 \vp 66\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*See how he writhes himself, and speaks no word.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 67 \vp 67\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And the other, who so stalwart seems, is Cassius.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 68 \vp 68\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*But night is reascending, and ’tis time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 69 \vp 69\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That we depart, for we have seen the whole.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 70 \vp 70\vp* As seemed him good, I clasped him round the neck,
\q1 \v 71 \vp 71\vp* And he the vantage seized of time and place,
\q1 \v 72 \vp 72\vp* And when the wings were opened wide apart,
\b
\q1 \v 73 \vp 73\vp* He laid fast hold upon the shaggy sides;
\q1 \v 74 \vp 74\vp* From fell to fell descended downward then
\q1 \v 75 \vp 75\vp* Between the thick hair and the frozen crust.
\b
\q1 \v 76 \vp 76\vp* When we were come to where the thigh revolves
\q1 \v 77 \vp 77\vp* Exactly on the thickness of the haunch,
\q1 \v 78 \vp 78\vp* The Guide, with labour and with hard-drawn breath,
\b
\q1 \v 79 \vp 79\vp* Turned round his head where he had had his legs,
\q1 \v 80 \vp 80\vp* And grappled to the hair, as one who mounts,
\q1 \v 81 \vp 81\vp* So that to Hell I thought we were returning.
\b
\q1 \v 82 \vp 82\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Keep fast thy hold, for by such stairs as these,”\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 83 \vp 83\vp* The Master said, panting as one fatigued,
\q1 \v 84 \vp 84\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Must we perforce depart from so much evil.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 85 \vp 85\vp* Then through the opening of a rock he issued,
\q1 \v 86 \vp 86\vp* And down upon the margin seated me;
\q1 \v 87 \vp 87\vp* Then tow’rds me he outstretched his wary step.
\b
\q1 \v 88 \vp 88\vp* I lifted up mine eyes and thought to see
\q1 \v 89 \vp 89\vp* Lucifer in the same way I had left him;
\q1 \v 90 \vp 90\vp* And I beheld him upward hold his legs.
\b
\q1 \v 91 \vp 91\vp* And if I then became disquieted,
\q1 \v 92 \vp 92\vp* Let stolid people think who do not see
\q1 \v 93 \vp 93\vp* What the point is beyond which I had passed.
\b
\q1 \v 94 \vp 94\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Rise up,”\zsay-e\* the Master said, \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“upon thy feet;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 95 \vp 95\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The way is long, and difficult the road,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 96 \vp 96\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And now the sun to middle-tierce returns.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 97 \vp 97\vp* It was not any palace corridor
\q1 \v 98 \vp 98\vp* There where we were, but dungeon natural,
\q1 \v 99 \vp 99\vp* With floor uneven and unease of light.
\b
\q1 \v 100 \vp 100\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“Ere from the abyss I tear myself away,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 101 \vp 101\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*My Master,”\zsay-e\* said I when I had arisen,
\q1 \v 102 \vp 102\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*“To draw me from an error speak a little;\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 103 \vp 103\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Where is the ice? and how is this one fixed\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 104 \vp 104\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*Thus upside down? and how in such short time\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 105 \vp 105\vp* \zsay-s |who="Dante"\*From eve to morn has the sun made his transit?”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 106 \vp 106\vp* And he to me: \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*“Thou still imaginest\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 107 \vp 107\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou art beyond the centre, where I grasped\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 108 \vp 108\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The hair of the fell worm, who mines the world.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 109 \vp 109\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*That side thou wast, so long as I descended;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 110 \vp 110\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*When round I turned me, thou didst pass the point\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 111 \vp 111\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To which things heavy draw from every side,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 112 \vp 112\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And now beneath the hemisphere art come\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 113 \vp 113\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Opposite that which overhangs the vast\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 114 \vp 114\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Dry-land, and ’neath whose cope was put to death\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 115 \vp 115\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*The Man who without sin was born and lived.\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 116 \vp 116\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Thou hast thy feet upon the little sphere\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 117 \vp 117\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Which makes the other face of the Judecca.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 118 \vp 118\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Here it is morn when it is evening there;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 119 \vp 119\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And he who with his hair a stairway made us\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 120 \vp 120\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Still fixed remaineth as he was before.\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 121 \vp 121\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Upon this side he fell down out of heaven;\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 122 \vp 122\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And all the land, that whilom here emerged,\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 123 \vp 123\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*For fear of him made of the sea a veil,\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 124 \vp 124\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*And came to our hemisphere; and peradventure\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 125 \vp 125\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*To flee from him, what on this side appears\zsay-e\*
\q1 \v 126 \vp 126\vp* \zsay-s |who="Virgil"\*Left the place vacant here, and back recoiled.”\zsay-e\*
\b
\q1 \v 127 \vp 127\vp* A place there is below, from Beelzebub
\q1 \v 128 \vp 128\vp* As far receding as the tomb extends,
\q1 \v 129 \vp 129\vp* Which not by sight is known, but by the sound
\b
\q1 \v 130 \vp 130\vp* Of a small rivulet, that there descendeth
\q1 \v 131 \vp 131\vp* Through chasm within the stone, which it has gnawed
\q1 \v 132 \vp 132\vp* With course that winds about and slightly falls.
\b
\q1 \v 133 \vp 133\vp* The Guide and I into that hidden road
\q1 \v 134 \vp 134\vp* Now entered, to return to the bright world;
\q1 \v 135 \vp 135\vp* And without care of having any rest
\b
\q1 \v 136 \vp 136\vp* We mounted up, he first and I the second,
\q1 \v 137 \vp 137\vp* Till I beheld through a round aperture
\q1 \v 138 \vp 138\vp* Some of the beauteous things that Heaven doth bear;
\b
\q1 \v 139 \vp 139\vp* Thence we came forth to rebehold the stars.
